N2  - Background: Recent advancements in treatment modalities and subsequent improvement in mortality for patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) have been afforded by targeted immunotherapy.1–4 Identification of therapeutic targets have been made possible by immunohistochemistry and more recently, genomic analysis through next generation sequencing (NGS). It is known that endobronchial ultrasound (EBUS) to mediastinal lymph nodes can be a good source of tissue for NGS. However, data is sparse in evaluating peripheral nodule biopsy for NGS. We aimed to evaluate our adequacy for NGS via robotic-assisted bronchoscopy (RB), a newer diagnostic platform, to guide treatment management. Methods: In this single-center, prospective, and observational study, medical records of patients undergoing diagnostic RB for peripheral pulmonary nodule (PPN) evaluation at UIH between January 2022 and January 2023 were reviewed for analysis. Inclusion criteria included patients ages 18 or older deemed appropriate for elective RB who were ultimately diagnosed with NSCLC on peripheral lung biopsy. We subsequently evaluated those patients for adequate tumor cellularity for NGS and if so, presence of actional mutation(s). Results: Of 56 nodules that were targeted, 73.2% of the procedures were diagnostic and 82.4% of the malignant diagnoses were NSCLC. 88% of the NSCLC cases on which NGS was requested were sufficient for testing, of which 59.1% had actionable mutation(s) detected. Further, 80.5% of patients required only one procedure for diagnosis and staging, promoting a quicker transition to treatment. Conclusions: In the era of targeted immunotherapy for stage III or IV lung cancer, as well as its rising role as part of neoadjuvant therapy for early-stage lung NSCLC, an important consideration for a diagnostic procedure includes whether the results were able to guide management through evaluation of NGS for targetable mutations. In this cohort, 88% of the applicable cases were sufficient for NGS analysis, and in our broader cohort, one procedure (combined RB and EBUS under one anesthesia event) was sufficient for full staging in 80.5% of patients. While further studies are needed, the potential ability of RB to assist with navigation to PPN without compromise of diagnostic yield, perform staging, and obtain sufficient tissue to guide targeted therapy as a single procedure makes it an attractive diagnostic modality. 1. Forde PM et al. N Engl J Med. 2022;386(21):1973-1985. 2. Chaft JE et al. Nat Med. 2022;28(10):2155-2161. 3. Wislez M et al. J Immunother Cancer. 2022;10(10):e005636. 4. Cascone T et al. Nat Med. 2021;27(3):504-514.
