N2  - Introduction Stage IIIA N2 NSCLC presents a significant clinical challenge, with suboptimal survival rates despite therapeutic advancements, highlighting the need for more effective treatments. Methods This non-randomised retrospective study analysed 143 consecutive T1/2 N2 (TNM 8th edition) NSCLC patients, treated with curative intent, from 2015 to 2021. Patients were divided into: Group A (30 patients) underwent surgery for clinical N2; Group B (59 patients) received (chemo)radiotherapy for clinical N2; and Group C (54 patients) had unsuspected N2 disease at surgery. PET +/- EBUS (73%) was used to diagnose clinical N2 disease. Median follow-up was 26 months. Results In Group A, 73% had single-station and 27% had multi-station N2 disease at surgery, with 20% receiving neoadjuvant and 50% adjuvant treatment. Recurrence rate was 63%. In Group B, 47 underwent chemoradiotherapy and 12 had radiotherapy alone; 6 received adjuvant Durvalumab. Among them, 71% had single-station N2 disease. Recurrence rate was 66%. Comparing surgery and (chemo) radiotherapy (Groups A + C Vs B), median overall survival (OS) was 36 versus 25 months (p = 0.785). Disease-free survival (DFS) was 20 versus 17 months (p = 0.878). In Group C, where unsuspected N2 disease was found at surgery, EBUS was used with PET in 15%. Adjuvant treatments were administered to 74%, with a recurrence rate of 70%. When assessing each group individually (Group Avs B vs C), median OS was 32 vs 25 vs 36 months (p = 0.746), and median DFS was 16 vs 17 vs 22 months (p = 0.802). [Formula presented] Conclusion This study provides a historical baseline for traditional treatments in T1/2 N2 NSCLC. No significant differences in DFS or OS were observed among patient groups. This data is vital for assessing emerging therapies, like neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy for resectable disease and Durvalumab after chemoradiotherapy. These insights could inform future therapy strategies, aiding efforts to improve NSCLC treatment outcomes. Disclosure No significant relationships.
