N2  - Background Checkmate 816 has established a new standard of care for operable NSCLC, combining neoadjuvant nivolumab with 3 cycles of platinum-based chemotherapy, granted approval in the UK in March 2023. This represents a shift in practice from traditional adjuvant treatment, with implications for patient care and service delivery. [Formula presented] Methods We conducted a retrospective analysis of patients receiving neoadjuvant nivolumab plus platinum doublet chemotherapy at our centre from March–December 2023. Primary objective was additional unplanned clinical contacts, secondary objectives including safety and surgical outcomes. Results 13 patients included, 10/13 (76.9%) adenocarcinoma; 3/13 (23.1%) squamous cell carcinoma. 6/13 (46.1%) stage IIB, 3/13 (23.1%) stage IIIA and 4/13 (30.8%) stage IIIB. All patients had documented operable status discussed in MDT at baseline; 50% of patients had both PDL1, ALK and EGFR available to clinician at time of first oncology consultation. Face to face surgical review was conducted in 5/13 (38.5%) at baseline, 2/13 (15.4%) after cycle 2, and 6/13 (46.1%) after cycle 3. None had grade 3/4 toxicity; 4/13 (30.7%) required dose reduction and additional 9 unplanned contacts in total were required (4 additional clinic reviews, 5 additional blood tests).12/13 (92.3%) underwent surgery within 6 weeks of treatment completion, 1 patient had disease progression and did not proceed to surgery. Of operated patients there were no unexpected post-operative complications. 6/12 (50%) had VATS while remainder had open thoracotomy. Compared to pre-operative staging, 6/12 (50%) were downstaged pathologically of which 2/12 (16.7%) had pCR; 2/12 (16.7%) had unchanged staging and 4/12 (33.3%) were upstaged. 1 patient had adjuvant radiotherapy for R1 resection. Conclusion Real world data in our centre is comparable with published trial data, although pathway issues around availability of biomarker data and timing of surgical review were highlighted. There was no grade 3–4 toxicity and a small increase in unplanned clinical activity. Disclosure No significant relationships.
