N2  - Introduction Nice recommendations from March 2023 advocate the use of neoadjuvant nivolumab with chemotherapy in adults with resectable node positive NSCLC or NSCLC ≥4 cm, based on clinical effectiveness from CheckMate 816. We present our early experience of operated patients following introduction of this pathway at our unit. Methods All eligible patients were discussed at lung cancer multidisciplinary team meetings and reviewed by Oncology and Thoracic teams at diagnosis. All operated patients were included in our series. Data was collected from electronic hospital records. Results 10 patients underwent surgery between May 2023 and January 2024 across 4 thoracic surgeons. 7 patients had tumours ≥4 cm, 1 patient had single station N2 disease and 2 patients had tumours ≥4 cm and N2 disease. 70% were male with a median age of 69 (range 48 to 77). 70% of tumours were squamous cell carcinoma and 30% adenocarcinoma. 90% of patients had immunotherapy with nivolumab in combination with 2 chemotherapy agents. Restaging was performed prior to surgical resection with complete response noted in 20%, partial in 40% and stable disease in 40% of patients using the RECIST criteria. 50% of cases were open from the start with 1 thoracotomy conversion due to bleeding (Clavien-Dindo IV). 9 patients had a lobectomy, with 1 requiring pneumonectomy. Median length of stay was 7 days for open procedures, and 4 days for minimally invasive procedures, higher than our unit averages. 40% of patients had <10% tumour cells on final histopathology. All patients achieved R0 resection. Conclusions Evaluating our early experience of this pathway upholds patient safety allowing reflection of anaesthetic and surgical considerations for this group of patients. Our data shows favourable short-term oncological outcomes and safety with 0% mortality reported to date. Only 40% of cases were performed by minimally invasive techniques compared to the unit average of 79% for lung resections. Disclosure No significant relationships.
