N2  - Introduction: Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) are a key component of both adjuvant and neoadjuvant treatment of resectable lung cancer. The current meta-analysis was performed to compare the survival outcomes between ICI(s) versus CMT alone for the treatment of resectable NSCLC administered in the neoadjuvant or adjuvant setting. Methods: Embase, Medline, and CENTRAL databases were used for screening articles with search terms related to NSCLC and ICI. Eligible studies were randomized controlled trials that included patients with early-stage, resectable NSCLC with at least one arm receiving ICI with CMT and one arm receiving CMT alone as neoadjuvant or adjuvant therapy. The study must report overall survival (OS) or disease-free survival (DFS). Effect estimates and their 95% confidence interval (CI) were extracted from each study using the generic-inverse variance method to determine the combined pooled hazard ratio (HR). Results: A total of 9,375 articles were identified from the search strategy. In total, four studies were included in the meta-analysis. Both OS and DFS were improved for patients receiving neoadjuvant CMT + ICI versus CMT alone, pooled HR 0.53; 95%CI 0.36-0.78 and 0,60; 95% CI 0.44-0.80, respectively. For adjuvant CMT + ICI, the OS benefit was not statistically significant, pooled HR 0.76; 95% CI 0.78-1.12 but improvement in DFS was significant, pooled HR 0.77 95% CI 0.67-0.89. Significantly improved OS was noted in the programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) positive subgroup (pooled HR 0.64; 0.51-0.81) and non-squamous histology (pooled HR 0.68; 95%CI 0.58-0.81). The addition of ICI to CMT did not significantly improve OS in PD-L1 negative and squamous histology tumors (Figure 1). EGFR mutational status did not impact DFS or OS for adjuvant CMT + ICI. Conclusions: Neoadjuvant and adjuvant ICI therapy improves OS and DFS in patients with early-stage, resectable NSCLC, especially those with positive PD-L1 status and non-squamous histology. Further ongoing trials involving both neoadjuvant and adjuvant ICIs are needed to refine the role of ICIs in this clinical scenario. [Formula presented] Keywords: Immune checkpoint inhibitor, Neoadjuvant, Adjuvant
