N2  - Introduction: Treatment with neoadjuvant nivolumab plus chemotherapy has demonstrated high efficacy in patients with locally advanced non-small-cell lung cancer (NSCLC). However, limited information is available regarding the influence of tumor molecular alterations on survival outcomes. BRAF mutations affect a small but significant fraction of NSCLC patients. However, the efficacy of these treatments in this set of tumors is unknown. Thus, we performed a retrospective study comparing the survival outcomes of patients with BRAF-mutated and BRAF-wt tumors in NADIM clinical trials. Methods: Samples from resectable stage IIIA/B NSCLC patients included in NADIM (n=46) and NADIMII (only experimental arm; n=46) clinical trials were used (n=92 patients). In particular, we analyzed the baseline plasma samples from 82 patients and 59 FFPE pre-treatment biopsy samples. Circulating nucleic acids were analyzed by next-generation sequencing (NGS) using the Oncomine Pan-Cancer Cell-Free Assay (NADIM cohort) on an Ion S5 sequencer (Thermofisher) or TruSight™ Oncology 500 ctDNA panel (NADIM II cohort) on a NovaSeq (Illumina). FFPE samples were analyzed using the Oncomine Tumor Mutation Load Assay (NADIM and NADIM II cohorts) on the Ion S5 sequencer. Variant filtering was performed using an optimized script developed in our laboratory. Oncomine variants files were obtained with the Ion Reporter analysis platform (Thermofisher). NGS data generated with the Illumina platform were analyzed with the DRAGEN TSO 500 ctDNA Analysis Software v1.2 using the TSO 500 pipeline. Survival statistical analysis was performed using RStudio and Stata version 17.0. Results: In NADIM trials, pathogenic or likely pathogenic genetic alterations in the BRAF gene occurred in 4.35% of the patients (n=4; p.L597Q, p.G469V, p.G464, p.V600E). BRAF pathogenic mutations were significantly enriched in tumors showing pathological complete response after neoadjuvant treatment with chemo-immunotherapy. Specifically, pCR rate in patients whose tumors were BRAF-positive (n=4) was 100%, whereas the pCR rate in the BRAF-wt population was 44.3% (n=39) (RR: 2.26; 95%CI 1.79-2.85). Likewise, all patients whose tumors harbored these alterations were alive and with no evidence of disease at the data cutoff (median follow-up: 35.1 months; IQR: 29.47-3.27months). Progression-free survival and overall survival probabilities at 3-years were 60.5% and 76.1%, respectively, for patients with BRAF-wt tumors. Finally, we did not find any association between BRAF mutation status and clinicopathological characteristics, namely sex, age, histology, ECOG and smoking history. Conclusions: Our data suggest that BRAF pathogenic variants may be a good prognostic factor in locally advance NSCLC patients treated with neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy. Keywords: NADIM, BRAF, NEOADJUVANT CHEMO-INMUNOTHERAPY
