N2  - Introduction: Inconsistent pathological responses of the primary tumor and lymph nodes (LNs) were frequently observed in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients receiving neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy. However, there is a lack of studies to report the prognostic significance and the relevant clinicopathological factors of tumor-nodal inconsistent responses after neoadjuvant immunotherapy or chemoimmunotherapy. Therefore, this study aimed to depict the inconsistent pathological combined tumor-nodal response in NSCLC patients after neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy as well as the underlying prognostic significance. Methods: A total of 81 node-positive NSCLC patients who underwent neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy were eligible for inclusion. Patient demographics and radiological and pathological features were recorded. Based on the degree of pathologic response of the primary tumor and lymph nodes, we further classified patients with pathological complete response of both tumor (ypT(pCR+)) and LNs (ypN0) into the combined well-responder group and the relevant clinicopathological features were evaluated. The event-free survival (EFS) outcome was analyzed using Kaplan-Meier analysis. Results: The ypN0 and ypT(pCR) rates were 74.1% and 42.0%, respectively. There was a significant association between ypT(pCR) and ypN0 (P = 0.003, Figure A), but inconsistent responses remained. Significantly improved EFS was observed in ypT(pCR+)/ypN0 cohort than that in the cohorts where inconsistent tumor-nodal responses occurred (P < 0.001, Figure B). The AUCs of pathological combined response (1-year AUC = 0.618, 95%CI = 0.495-0.878; 3-year AUC = 0.763, 95%CI = 0.426-1.000) were generally comparable to the ypTNM stage (1-year AUC = 0.687, 95%CI = 0.519-0.893; 3-year AUC = 0.742, 95%CI = 0.426-1.000). Patients who received more than two infusions of immune checkpoint inhibitors after 15:30 had a poorer survival outcome (P=0.015). Conclusions: The inconsistent tumor-nodal response was observed in more than 70% of NSCLC patients receiving neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy. Significantly improved EFS was observed in patients who achieved combined good tumor-nodal responses. The combined pathological responses of primary tumors and nodal may be used as a surrogate for the ypTNM staging system. Keywords: Non-small cell lung carcinoma, Neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy, Pathological response [Formula presented]
