N2  - Introduction: In patients aged ≥65 years with resectable non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), selecting effective and safe neoadjuvant treatment (chemotherapy vs immunotherapy [IO] vs chemotherapy+IO) is a major challenge. Also, the diverse T-cell receptor (TCR) repertoire crucial for a robust anti-cancer immune response changes with age. We present a subgroup analysis of clinical outcomes by age and TCR repertoire from LCMC3 (NCT02927301), a Phase II study of neoadjuvant and adjuvant atezolizumab (anti-PD-L1) in NSCLC that met its primary endpoint: a 20% major pathological response (MPR) rate after neoadjuvant atezolizumab. Methods: Patients with stage IB-IIIA or select IIIB NSCLC, ≥18 years of age and ECOG PS 0-1 received neoadjuvant atezolizumab 1200 mg IV every 3 weeks (q3w) for ≤2 cycles followed by surgery. Patients could receive adjuvant atezolizumab q3w for ≤12 months. MPR rate and safety were assessed. Tumour tissue TCR sequencing (immunoSEQ, Adaptive Biotechnologies) was performed to determine Simpson clonality and richness. Patients were analysed by age (<65 years and ≥65 years). Results: Data cutoff: Oct 21, 2022. Baseline characteristics and comorbidities with a ≥15% difference between groups (<65 years vs ≥65 years) in enrolled NSCLC patients (N=181) are below (Table). 90% (<65 years) vs 86% (≥65 years; P=0.489) had surgical resection. Median T-cell clonality pre-treatment (n=102) in non-squamous was significantly lower in <65 years vs ≥65 years (0.06 vs 0.09; P=0.003) and in squamous was higher (0.12 vs 0.08; P=0.031). After atezolizumab treatment (n=108), this trend did not persist in non-squamous (0.11 vs 0.13; P=0.303) or squamous (0.11 vs 0.16; P=0.189). In patients without MPR, the change in pre- to post-treatment T-cell clonality was lower in squamous for <65 years vs ≥65 years (n=25; −0.09 vs 0.80; P=0.004), and the change in richness was lower in non-squamous (n=40; −0.06 vs 0.01; P=0.032). In the primary efficacy population (PEP, n=143), both age groups had a similar MPR rate (20%) In the PEP with MPR assessed (n=137), 3-year OS rates for <65 years vs ≥65 years were 88% vs 77% (HR, 1.98; 95% CI: 0.82, 4.77; P=0.128). All patient (N=181) safety and median hospitalisation duration are below (Table). Conclusions: In patients with resectable NSCLC, the MPR rate with atezolizumab treatment was similar between age groups. Our study found differences between groups in T-cell clonality and richness that should be further investigated. Although we saw numerical differences between groups for toxicities and hospitalization duration, there was no difference in 3-year OS. [Formula presented] Keywords: NSCLC, age, T-cell receptor
