N2  - Introduction: Precision oncology has emerged as a groundbreaking approach to the management of lung cancer, revolutionizing the way treatments are tailored to individual patients. However, a significant challenge remains in ensuring equitable access to advanced testing for detecting driver mutations, particularly in developing countries. Limited resources, logistic issues, and disparities in healthcare systems often hinder the widespread adoption of molecular profiling techniques in these regions. Evaluating every step of the process is fundamental to understand its limitations and where are the missed opportunities. Methods: In this retrospective study, we aimed to assess the missed opportunities for precision oncology in lung cancer management at various stages of the process, focusing on patients with advanced and early non-squamous non-small cell lung cancer (NSq NSCLC). We reviewed the electronic medical records (EMRs) of 174 patients and included a total of 103 patients (25 early-stage and 78 advanced-stage) with NSq NSCLC. The evaluation of missed opportunities was conducted across consecutive key steps: access to genomic testing, exam feasibility, availability of test results for all recommended biomarkers, and access to targeted therapy for patients with actionable gene mutations. Results: For patients with early-stage NSq NSCLC (n=25), we missed 80% of patients in the first step (access to genomic testing). Among patients with advanced disease (n=78), we missed 32% of the patients that were classified as unable to undergo to genomic testing, primarily due to a short life expectancy that did not allow for the time required to wait for the test. Among 53 patients who had access to testing, 9% were missed due to insufficient sample for analysis. 48 patients (65% of the study population) obtained results from genomic testing, but when considering all recommended tests, 98% of patients were missed, as only one participant underwent testing for all biomarkers. When considering only EGFR and ALK testing, 46% of patients were missed, and when considering only EGFR testing, only 6% of patients were missed. Regarding missed opportunities for targeted therapy in lung cancer, 40% of the 20 patients with actionable gene mutations did not receive targeted therapy. However, when considering only patients with EGFR-mutated tumors (n = 12), only 8% of patients were missed due to the lack of access to anti-EGFR therapy. Conclusions: Our study highlights the critical challenges and missed opportunities in implementing precision oncology for lung cancer management, particularly in resource-limited settings. The results for early-stage disease may represent a challenge considering emerging therapies such as neo/adjuvant immunotherapy or targeted-therapy. Our results emphasize the need to improve access to genomic testing, ensure adequate sample for analysis, and expand the testing panel for recommended biomarkers. Moreover, we identified barriers to the adoption of targeted therapy for patients with actionable gene mutations besides EGFR. Keywords: Genomic Testing, Precision Oncology, EGFR-mutated NSCLC
