N2  - Introduction: Immune checkpoint inhibitors have greatly revolutionized the treatment of non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). Besides better pathological regression, recent researches demonstrated that patients accepting preoperative immunochemotherapy could obtain better prognosis. Nevertheless, in the patients achieving major pathological response (MPR) or even pathologic complete response (pCR), recrudescent diseases arose. This investigation was conducted for figuring out the prognostic value of combined immunotherapy in neoadjuvant therapy and exploring the optimal treatment regimens. Methods: Patients undergoing neoadjuvant immunochemotherapy or platinum-containing chemotherapy followed by surgery from June 2018 to June 2022 was retrospectively enrolled. Recrudescent diseases limited within ipsilateral hemithorax and regional lymph nodes including hilar, mediastinal and cervical lymph nodes were classified as locoregional recurrence. Distant recurrence was defined as occurring in the contralateral lung or outside the hemithorax of the primary tumor. Pathological response, recurrence pattern, recurrence-free survival (RFS) and overall survival (OS) were compared between the regimens. Results: A total of 214 patients met inclusion criteria, including 108 accepting neoadjuvant immunochemotherapy and 106 accepting platinum-containing chemotherapy. Baseline characteristics were of no significant difference and omitted here. Patients treated with immunotherapy and chemotherapy shared similar rates of R0 resection (101/108 vs. 95/106) and postoperative death within 30 days (2/108 vs. 1/106). Combination group showed remarkably higher MPR (57/108 vs 12/106, P<0.001) and pCR (35/108 vs 4/106, P<0.001) rates. Analysis for recurrence and survival was conducted in 194 patients, excluding those who got R1/2 resection or died within 30 days. The results for both RFS (HR, 0.37; 95% CI, 0.23-0.59) and OS (HR, 0.19; 95% CI, 0.07-0.47) favored immunochemotherapy over chemotherapy (Figure-1A). Besides the higher recurrence rates (Figure-1B), patients accepting pure chemotherapy were more likely to have distant or complex recurrence (Figure-1C), which was related to poorer OS (Figure-1D). Consistent with previous investigations, we found the MPR trended toward improved RFS (HR, 0.18; 95% CI, 0.11-0.29) and OS (HR, 0.24; 95% CI, 0.09-0.64) (Figure-1E). Although the MPR was not reached, patients accepting immunochemotherapy had potentially better RFS (HR, 0.60; 95% CI, 0.36-1.00) and OS (HR, 0.34; 95% CI, 0.12-0.97) (Figure-1F). Further results revealed that patients who did not obtain MPR could benefit from adjuvant therapy (HR, 0.26; 95% CI, 0.07-0.98; Figure-1G). Conclusions: For patients with resectable NSCLC, immunotherapy brought better pathological response, lower recurrence rates, more limited recurrence than chemotherapy alone. Although pathological regression did not reach MPR, combined application of immunotherapy could prolong the RFS and OS. [Formula presented] Keywords: Neoadjuvant therapy, Immunotherapy, Resectable NSCLC
