N2  - Introduction: The use of immunotherapy in the treatment of metastatic lung cancer has revolutionized patient care, significantly improving survival rates. Studies have demonstrated a strong correlation between PD-L1 levels and overall, recurrence-free survival in patients receiving immunotherapy for metastatic NSCLC. Building on the success of these studies, immunotherapy has moved to early stages, improving event free survival and, probably, overall survival in patients. We sought to analyze our own clinical experience of neoadjuvant Ch-IO and evaluate the role of PD-L1 expression as a predictive marker of achieving pCR and MPR in a real world clinical practice of a large community based comprehensive cancer center. Methods: We conducted a real world analysis of the first patient cases who underwent neoadjuvant Ch-IO and definitive surgery in our center from November 2019 to April 2023 In this abstract, we present data on the first 61 patients who underwent definitive surgery after neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy (Ch-IO). Prior to therapy, all patients underwent biopsy and PD-L1 expression and activating mutations were analyzed. Patients with activating mutations were excluded, resulting in 34 patients in the squamous cell group and 27 in the non-squamous non-small cell group. All patients received 3-6 cycles of neoadjuvant Ch-IO, and treatment response was assessed after 3 and 6 cycles. A multidisciplinary team discussed each patient to determine surgical treatment. Postoperative histological material was assessed according to IASLC guidelines for pathological examination of lung cancer after neoadjuvant treatment by William D. Travis (2020). Results: Radiological partial response was achieved in 58 patients (95%), 2 patients (3.3%) had stable disease and 1 patient progressed. All patients underwent surgical treatment. 45 patients (74%) received lobectomy with LN dissection, 9 patients (14.5%) underwent pneumonectomy with LN dissection, 7 patients (11.5%) underwent bilobectomy with LN dissection. According to postoperative histological examination, the best results were achieved in the group of patients (10 cases) with squamous cell lung cancer with PD-L1 expression over 50%: 8 patients achieved pCR (80%), 2 patients achieved MPR (20%). In patients with Sq NSCLC with PD-L1 1-49% (13 cases), 5 patients (38.5%) achieved pCR, 1 patient (7.7%) achieved MPR, and the remaining 7 patients did not achieve MPR or pCR. In the group with PD-L1 levels less than 1% (8 cases), 3 cases (37.5%) achieved pCR, and 3 cases (37.5%) achieved MPR. Among the non-squamous non-small cell carcinoma group with PD-L1 levels above 50% (9 cases), 6 cases (66.7%) achieved pCR, and 2 cases (22.3%) achieved MPR. In the PD-L1 level 1-49% group (6 cases), 2 patients (33.3%) achieved pCR, and 1 patient (16.7%) achieved MPR. In the group with PD-L1 levels less than 1% (12 cases), 6 patients (50%) achieved pCR, 1 patient (8.3%) achieved MPR Conclusions: Neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy leads to the achievement of pCR and MPR, resulting in improved EFS and ultimately OS. Notably, patients with squamous cell lung cancer with PD-L> 50% benefit the most from this treatment approach, achieving a 80% pCR and 100% MPR rate. This study has limitations and further research is necessary to confirm these findings. Keywords: Neoadjuvant therapy, Chemo-immunotherapy, pCR
