N2  - Introduction: The management of resectable locally advanced non-small cell lung cancer (LA-NSCLC) is controversial in the immunotherapy era. CheckMate 816 demonstrated prolonged event-free survival (EFS) and higher pathologic complete response (pCR) among patients receiving chemo-immunotherapy compared to chemotherapy alone. EFS was significantly higher in patients with pCR. No study to date has directly compared pathologic outcomes of neoadjuvant chemo-radiotherapy (a standard-of-care since publication of the INT0139 trial) and chemo-immunotherapy. In this analysis, we investigate practice patterns and pCR following neoadjuvant chemo-radiotherapy, chemo-immunotherapy, and chemotherapy. Methods: We analyzed the nationwide Flatiron Health database, a de-identified database derived from electronic health records. Adult patients were included in the study if they were diagnosed with stage II-IIIA NSCLC on or after January 1, 2019, completed surgery by February 1, 2023, and underwent at least 2 cycles of platinum-based neoadjuvant chemotherapy before confirmed surgical resection with available pCR data. We evaluated patient clinical and demographic characteristics associated with type of neoadjuvant therapy using the chi-square test. We evaluated associations between type of neoadjuvant therapy and pCR using univariable and multivariable logistic regression, adjusting for sex, diagnosis year, and ECOG. Results: The cohort included 47 patients (17 chemotherapy, 16 chemo-radiotherapy, 14 chemo-immunotherapy). The proportion of patients receiving neoadjuvant chemotherapy or chemo-radiotherapy decreased from 2019 to 2022 (47.1% to 12.5% and 47.1% to 6.3%, respectively), while chemo-immunotherapy increased from 5.9% to 81.3%. Sex was associated with neoadjuvant therapy receipt, with 87.5% of chemo-immunotherapy recipients being male compared to 42.9% of chemo-radiotherapy and 70.6% of chemotherapy recipients (p=0.031). For patients receiving chemo-radiotherapy and chemo-immunotherapy, pCR was achieved in 42.9% and 37.5%, respectively. Compared to chemotherapy, there was a statistically non-significant trend towards higher pCR for chemo-radiotherapy (odds ratio [OR] 5.62, 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.92-34.6, p=0.062) and chemo-immunotherapy (OR 4.50, 95% CI 0.75-26.9, p=0.099). There was no significant difference in pCR rate of chemo-radiotherapy compared with chemo-immunotherapy (OR 1.25, 95% CI 0.29-5.41, p=0.77). Multivariable analysis showed similar findings: chemo-radiotherapy versus chemotherapy (OR 14.7, 95% CI 1.15-189, p=0.039), chemo-immunotherapy versus chemotherapy (OR 15.3, 95% CI 0.86-271, p=0.063), and chemo-radiotherapy versus chemo-immunotherapy (OR 1.14, 95% CI 0.06-20.9, p=0.93). Conclusions: Preliminary data suggest that pCR may be comparable between neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy and chemo-radiotherapy. As neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy becomes more common, further analysis of pathologic response and survival following neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy and chemo-radiotherapy stratified by PD-L1 status may help inform clinical decision-making for patients with resectable LA-NSCLC. [Formula presented] Keywords: locally advanced non-small cell lung cancer, neoadjuvant chemoradiation, neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy
