N2  - Introduction: Several clinical trials have reported impressive results of neoadjuvant immunotherapy combined with chemotherapy in resectable non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). However, these well-designed clinical trials are far from sufficient to reflect the complicated situations in real clinical practice. Therefore, more real world studies are required to evaluate the efficacy and safety of neoadjuvant immunotherapy plus chemotherapy in stage resectable NSCLC. Methods: In this study, we conducted a real world retrospective analysis of 32 patients with resectable IIA-IIIB NSCLC, who had received neoadjuvant immunotherapy combined with chemotherapy in the Affiliated Hospital of Zunyi Medical University from November 2020 to November 2022. The neoadjuvant regimen was programmed cell death protein-1 (PD-1) inhibitors combined with platinum-based chemotherapy. The PD-1 inhibitors included sintilimab and tislelizumab and the treatment cycle was 2-4 cycles. The primary endpoint was major pathological response (MPR). The secondary endpoints included objective remission rate (ORR), pathological complete remission (pCR), clinical down staging rate and surgical outcomes. Results: A total of 32 patients received neoadjuvant immunotherapy combined with chemotherapy, including 17 patients with sintilimab and 15 patients with tislelizumab (Table 1). Postoperative pathology results showed that a total of 19 (59.38%) patients achieved MPR. The pCR was 50.00% (16/32) and ORR was 87.50% (28/32) (Table 2). The descending rate occurred in 28 of 32 patients (87.50%). 96.88% (31/32) patients underwent VATS switched to thoracotomy and R0 resection rate was 93.75% (30/32). The median surgery time was 215 min (130-340 min), 30-day and 90-day mortality were both 0% (Table 3). 73 adverse events(AEs)occurred during the treatment period, mainly grade 1-2 AEs (69/73, 94.52%). Conclusions: Our real-world study confirmed the feasibility and safety of neoadjuvant immunotherapy combined with chemotherapy in the treatment of resectable NSCLC. However, these findings still need prospective clinical trials to further confirm. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Keywords: Non-small cell lung cancer, Neoadjuvant therapy, PD-1 inhibitor
