N2  - Introduction: KRAS mutated NSCLC is associated with worse prognosis and response to systemic therapies. Neoadjuvant chemo or chemo/IO had been shown to improve MPR and OS. There is insufficient data to support its benefit in KRAS mutated NSCLC patients. In this single center retrospective analysis, we present outcomes of neoadjuvant therapies in resectable NSCLC patients with KRAS mutations. Methods: Patients (pts) with resectable NSCLC who received neoadjuvant chemotherapy (chemo) or chemo-immunotherapy (chemo/IO) at MD Anderson Cancer Center were identified from 1/2011 - 3/2021. Patient characteristics were summarized. Genomic testing was performed by CLIA-certified assays. We assessed major pathological response (MPR; ≤ 10% viable tumor), pathological complete response (pCR; 0% viable tumor), event-free survival (EFS) defined as from treatment to progression, recurrence or death and overall survival (OS) defined as from treatment to death in all patients and prespecified subgroups. MPR were compared between subgroups by Fisher’s exact test. Distributions of EFS and OS were compared between subgroups by logrank test. Results: 69 patients received chemo or chemo/IO for resectable KRAS-mutated NSCLC. Median age was 66; 57% were female; 42% of pts had KRAS G12C mutation; 15 pts (22%) received chemo/IO; 8 (12%) pts did not receive surgery. MPR was observed in 15 (22%) pts with 3 cases of pCR. Percentage of MPR was higher in chemo/IO pts compared with chemo (40% vs 17%, p=0.076) and in STK11 wild-type pts compared with mutated (25% vs 9%, p=0.433). Median EFS in all pts was 1.7 years (95% CI: 1.1-3.5). EFS was significantly longer in pts with MPR (p=0.002). With a median follow-up of 5.1 years, OS in all pts was 7.8 years (95% CI: 4.2-13.3); OS was significantly longer in pts with MPR (p=0.048) and KRAS G12C mutation trended towards better OS without statistical significance (p=0.072). Conclusions: Our analysis demonstrated neoadjuvant chemo/IO was associated with higher MPR in KRAS-mutated NSCLC pts than chemo alone. Achieving MPR prolonged EFS and OS. STK11 co-mutation may correlate with lower MPR percentage, shorter EFS and OS. TP53 co-mutation did not have clinically significant impact on MPR, EFS or OS. Future studies are needed to identify optimal neoadjuvant therapy for KRAS mutated NSCLC pts. [Formula presented] Keywords: Neoadjuvant, KRAS, NSCLC
