N2  - Introduction: The early non-small cell lung cancer (eNSCLC) treatment landscape and biomarker testing requirements are rapidly evolving. This study evaluated biomarker testing and treatment patterns among patients with eNSCLC in US clinical practice. Methods: This retrospective observational study used the nationwide de-identified electronic health record (EHR)-derived Flatiron Health research database. Eligible patients had ≥2 visits and an NSCLC diagnosis between Jan 1, 2019 and Aug 31, 2022, stage I-IIIB (AJCC 8th edition), any clinical activity in Flatiron within 90 days following diagnosis and no clinical trial participation. Clinical characteristics were extracted from EHR via natural language processing with machine learning (ML) for the primary analysis; results were validated using a human-abstracted dataset. Systemic treatment rates were evaluated among resected patients with ≥180 days of follow-up after surgery. Biomarker testing for PD-L1, ALK and EGFR was evaluated among resected stage II-IIIB patients. Utilization of atezolizumab, the only adjuvant immunotherapy approved in the US during the study period, was evaluated in a subset of patients eligible for atezolizumab. Results were summarized using descriptive statistics. Results: A total of 29,314 patients were included from the ML-extracted database. Median age was 72 years, 47% male, 64% White, 45% stage I, and 61% non-squamous. Surgery rates varied by stage: stage I (55%), stage II (56%), stage IIIA (30%) and stage IIIB (10%). Among resected patients (n=9174), 54% received surgery only, 40% adjuvant only, 2% neoadjuvant only and 4% neoadjuvant + adjuvant therapy. Biomarker testing rates and the timeliness of testing improved over time; however, many patients resected during the latest analysis quarter (Q1 2022) did not receive PD-L1 (17%), ALK (31%) or EGFR (19%) testing any time during the study or up to 90 days prior to a recurrence, if present (Figure). Among stage II-IIIB resected patients, 69% were PD-L1 ≥1%, 13% EGFR+, and 2% ALK+. Among stage II-IIIB patients resected between Jul 2021 and Feb 2022 receiving adjuvant chemotherapy, 22% did not have evidence of a PD-L1 test result before completing chemotherapy. Atezolizumab utilization among those eligible increased over time (overall rate, 55%). The validation analysis (N=2316) replicated findings from the ML-extracted dataset. Conclusions: This study presents early real-world data in the adjuvant eNSCLC setting after atezolizumab approval in this indication. Biomarker testing has increased, but potential gaps in biomarker-informed treatment decisions require further investigation. Replicated findings in human-abstracted data support the use of the ML-extracted dataset for eNSCLC real-world research. [Formula presented] Keywords: Biomarker testing, Machine Learning, early non-small cell lung cancer
