N2  - Introduction: Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs)-based neoadjuvant chemotherapy has been approved by the FDA to treat resectable NSCLC with significant benefit over chemotherapy alone. As the surrogate for endpoint of clinical benefit, the rate of pathological response is still limited to 24%. Currently, there are no clinical biomarkers to identify patients who are likely to achieve pathological complete response (pCR) from Immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs). We aimed to explore whether a novel multi-parameter biomarker based on radiomic features from pretreatment CT scans and clinical variables could predict pCR of NSCLC patients receiving neoadjuvant immunotherapy. Methods: CT scans from 141 pretreatment NSCLC patients receiving neoadjuvant immunotherapy (NAIT) between May 18, 2019 and April 26, 2022 were retrospectively collected and were randomized to training or validation cohorts using a 7:3 ratio. 851 features were extracted from both tumoral and each subregion of peri-tumoral VOIs. The reference standard is the pathological complete response (pCR) rate and the crucial exclusion criteria are missing pretreatment CT scans or unavailable surgical pathological result. After reproducibility analysis, radiomic signatures were derived implementing machine learning in the training cohort and were tested in the validation cohort. A clinical model using binary logistic regression analysis was constructed and incorporated into radiomic signature to improve performance further. Results: Pathological complete response was present in 47 patients (33%; training, 34%; validation, 32%). 851 radiomic features extracted from tumoral and peritumoral regions applied a novel sub-region segmentation strategy was then filtered by reproducibility re-test on RIDER dataset and redundancy test via LASSO algorithm. A Generalized Linear Model (GLM) included 11 features was selected as the final model yielding the highest training AUC of 0.89 (95% confidence interval (CI), 0.84-0.96) and validation AUC of 0.81 (0.66-0.97). With sensitivity of 82% and specificity of 78%, validation accuracy of the GLM was 0.79. This was significantly higher than the accuracy based on solitary tumoral radiomic signature (0.71; p<0.05) or clinical signature (type of platinum regimen, pathological type, derived neutrophil lymphocyte ratio (dNLR); 0.67; p<0.05). The performance of GLM was approved after incorporated with clinical signature in both training (AUC=0.91, 95% CI: 0.86-0.97) and validation sets (AUC=0.86, 95% CI: 0.72-0.99). Conclusions: Our study established a pragmatic tool applied a novel segmentation strategy to predict the pathological responses of NSCLC to NAIT. The model could assist clinicians in selection and adjustment of treatment regimens earlier and provide reference for post-NAIT surgery strategy for NSCLC. Keywords: Neoadjuvant immunotherapy, Radiomics, Peri-tumoral analysis [Formula presented]
