N2  - Introduction: An association between pCR and improved EFS has been reported in NSCLC patients treated with neoadjuvant immunotherapy alone or in combination; however, it is unclear if pCR can be considered a surrogate clinical endpoint for EFS. Methods: We performed a systematic literature review of trials reporting outcomes of neoadjuvant immune-based therapy on PubMed and the proceedings of the most relevant international conferences until November 2022. Hazard ratios (HRs) measuring the association between pCR and EFS were retrieved from all eligible trials and analyzed using a random-effects meta-analysis model. To evaluate surrogacy, Odds Ratios (ORs) for pCR and HRs for EFS were retrieved from all eligible randomized clinical trials (RCTs) and analyzed using a linear regression model weighted by sample size. R2 and linear regression slope were used respectively to estimate the proportion of variation in EFS effect explained by pCR effect and the magnitude of change in EFS effect as a function of the magnitude of change in pCR effect. Results: Four RCTs and 10 single-arm trials were identified for a total of 1030 pts. 3 out of 14 trials (255 pts) could be used for the correlation analysis between pCR and EFS. pCR was statistically associated with EFS (HR 0.23; 95%CI 0.11-0.47, p < 0.0001; I2 = 3%). Surrogacy analysis was performed on the four RCTs (548 pts). The proportion of variation in EFS effect was not well explained by pCR effect (R2 0.108; 95%CI 0-1) and EFS treatment effect increased of 0.16 points in terms of HR for each ten or points increase in pCR (p 0.672). Conclusions: Our results indicate a robust association between pCR and EFS benefit exists across multiple clinical trials, however surrogacy was not proven. This could be explained by the large and multifaceted heterogeneity, supporting strong efforts to harmonize and standardize trial procedures. [Formula presented] Keywords: Neoadjuvant immunotherapy, Non-small cell lung cancer, complete pathologic response
