N2  - Introduction: Although the standard treatment for most stage III NSCLC is chemoradiotherapy followed by durvalumab consolidation therapy, adding 45 Gy in 25 fractions of radiotherapy to neoadjuvant immunochemotherapy yields high pathological response for potentially resectable NSCLC, but with excess toxicity. Herein, a phase Ib study was designed to assess the safety and tolerability, especially DLT of low-dose radiotherapy (LDRT) plus durvalumab and chemotherapy as neoadjuvant therapy for potentially resectable stage III NSCLC, and to define the optimal radiotherapy dosage. Methods: In this open-label, single-arm, phase Ib study, eligible patients with histologically confirmed, potentially resectable stage III NSCLC were enrolled. Eligible patients received radiotherapy at escalating doses (10 Gy in 5 fractions [cohort 1], 20 Gy in 10 fractions [cohort 2], and 30 Gy in 15 fractions [cohort 3]) according to a 3+3 design, and concurrent 1500 mg durvalumab and chemotherapy (260 mg/m2 nab-paclitaxel plus carboplatin AUC=5) on day 1 for two 21-day cycles. Radical surgery was scheduled within 4-6 weeks after the last dose of neoadjuvant therapy, followed by adjuvant durvalumab (1500 mg, every 4 weeks) for up to 1 year. Primary endpoints were safety and tolerability. The secondary endpoints are ORR, EFS, pCR and MPR. Results: Between August 2021 and August 2022, 9 patients were enrolled, of which 100.0% achieved tumor downstaging after completion of planned neoadjuvant therapy. No DLT (grade≥3 radiation pneumonia or immune-associated pneumonia) was recorded. AEs of any grade occurred in all patients (Table 1). Grade 3-4 AEs were reported in 3 (33.3%) patients, including leukopenia (22.2%), anemia (11.1%), and infectious pneumonia (11.1%). Seven (77.8%) of 9 patients underwent successful R0 resection, with a median interval to surgery after neoadjuvant therapy of 44 days (30-60 days). No treatment-related surgical delay or death was observed. The MPR and pCR rates were both 100.0% (1/1) for cohort 1, 66.7% (2/3) and 0.0% for cohort 2, and 100.0% (3/3) and 66.7% (2/3) for cohort 3. The ORR was 66.7% for all three cohorts. At the data cutoff, event-free survival was immature. Conclusions: LDRT in combination with durvalumab and chemotherapy as neoadjuvant therapy was well tolerated in patients with potentially resectable stage III NSCLC. No DLT was reported, and the optimal radiotherapy dosage was determined to be 30 Gy in 15 fractions. Further researches are necessary to validate the preliminary safety and efficacy of the combination regimen in the present study. [Formula presented] Keywords: Low-dose radiotherapy, Neoadjuvant therapy, Durvalumab
