N2  - Introduction: First-line immunotherapy has been shown to significantly improve survival in patients with advanced/metastatic NSCLC. The anti-PD-1 antibody pembrolizumab alone showed superior efficacy over chemotherapy in untreated patients with advanced NSCLC and PD-L1 expression ≥50%, yet most patients will eventually experience progression. Datopotamab deruxtecan (Dato-DXd) is an antibody-drug conjugate composed of a humanized anti-TROP2 IgG1 monoclonal antibody covalently linked to a topoisomerase I inhibitor payload via a plasma-stable, tetrapeptide-based, cleavable linker. In the first-in-human phase 1 TROPION-PanTumor01 study (NCT03401385), Dato-DXd 6 mg/kg monotherapy demonstrated an objective response rate (ORR) of 28% and a manageable safety profile in pretreated patients with NSCLC. Preclinical studies provided a rationale for investigating Dato-DXd combined with anti-PD-1 and anti-PD-L1 antibody therapies. Subsequently, the phase 1b TROPION-Lung02 trial (NCT04526691) in patients with advanced NSCLC confirmed the tolerability of Dato-DXd 6 mg/kg combined with pembrolizumab, with stomatitis and nausea (mostly grade 1/2) as the most frequent treatment-emergent adverse events; the preliminary ORR (confirmed plus pending) for this combination as 1L therapy was 62% and the disease control rate was 100%. Here we describe TROPION-Lung08 (NCT05215340), a phase 3, global, randomized, open-label, active-controlled trial evaluating the efficacy and safety of Dato-DXd plus pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab alone in treatment-naive patients with advanced/metastatic NSCLC without actionable genomic alterations and PD-L1 expression ≥50% (as determined by central testing). Methods: Approximately 740 patients will be randomized 1:1 to receive Dato-DXd 6 mg/kg plus pembrolizumab 200 mg or pembrolizumab 200 mg alone via infusion every 21 days until disease progression, discontinuation, or 35 cycles of pembrolizumab. Randomization will be stratified by Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status (0 vs 1), histology (squamous vs nonsquamous), geographic region (East Asia vs rest of world), and smoking status (former/current vs never). Patients must have stage IIIB or IIIC NSCLC ineligible for curative treatment or stage IV disease and be naive to systemic therapy; if patients received neoadjuvant/adjuvant systemic therapy without an immune checkpoint inhibitor, treatment must have been completed ≥6 months before the diagnosis of advanced/metastatic disease. Patients with previously treated stable brain metastases can participate. Those with a history of noninfectious interstitial lung disease (ILD)/pneumonitis that required steroids or who have current/suspected ILD/pneumonitis will be excluded. The primary endpoints are progression-free survival (PFS) by blinded independent central review (BICR) per RECIST version 1.1 and overall survival, with target hazard ratios of 0.65 and 0.75, respectively. Secondary endpoints include BICR- and investigator-assessed ORR, investigator-assessed PFS, PFS2, duration of response, time to response, disease control rate, time to deterioration, safety, and antidrug antibody prevalence. Pharmacokinetic parameters, biomarkers, and patient-reported outcomes will be explored; study enrollment is ongoing. Keywords: Datopotamab deruxtecan, Pembrolizumab, Antibody-drug conjugate
