N2  - Introduction: Select patients with non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) may become candidates for curative resection following neoadjuvant immunotherapy, and recent reports of survival are encouraging compared with neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Whether the neoadjuvant immunotherapy increased the difficulty of the following surgery remains unclear. The aim of this study was to investigate the impact of neoadjuvant immunotherapy on real-world surgical practice of surgeons with NSCLC patients. Methods: An electronic survey was systematically distributed by Wechat specialised groups of thoracic surgeons from China. Data collected included personal information, management preferences, preoperative evaluation, intraoperative practice and postoperative events to assess surgical practice difference and difficulty after neoadjuvant immunotherapy. Results: A total of 267 eligible responses were received from 27 provinces of China. Most (72%) of these responders have practiced more than 10 years and 46% work in high-volume centers (>3000 cases/year). Central NSCLC of stage III is the most common setting for neoadjuvant immunotherapy. Tumor size, lymph node metastasis and probability of R0 resection are mostly considered for neoadjuvant immunotherapy. Most surgeons (81%) chose to perform surgery 4-6 weeks after neoadjuvant immunotherapy. Lobectomy (86%) and sleeve lobectomy (13%) are the most common type of surgery. Immune-related adverse events are observed by 45% of surgeons and higher in more experienced surgeons. Experience for length of surgery varied between responders: 37% chose longer, 30% chose comparable and 32% had no preference. For intraoperative bleeding consideration, 15% chose a greater risk for bleeding, 60% chose comparable and 21% had no preference. The opinion regarding difficulty of surgery also varied between responders: 32% chose an increased difficulty, 32% chose comparable and 29% believed that the hard one gets harder and the easy one gets easier. Most of the surgeons agrees that neoadjuvant immunotherapy did not increase the risk of intraoperative or postoperative major adverse events. Conclusions: In a multi-center survey of thoracic surgeons, there are some variations in management preferences of surgery. In general, neoadjuvant immunotherapy resulted in acceptable adverse events, enhanced the resectability and did not increase the complexity of surgery for most experienced surgeons. [Formula presented] Keywords: Non-small Cell Lung Cancer, Neoadjuvant Immunotherapy, Surgical Management
