N2  - Introduction: Optimal treatment for patients with Stage IIIA NSCLC is controversial. NCCN recommends resection of bulky local disease, definitive concurrent chemoradiation (DC) consolidated with immunotherapy for extensive nodal involvement, and neoadjuvant therapy to downstage everything in between. Growing evidence indicates resection in combination with chemotherapy before or after surgery (S+C) may be superior to DC. Direct comparisons between S+C and DC with immunotherapy (DCI), among patients with IIIA disease diagnosed since immunotherapy was added to treatment guidelines, have not been performed. Methods: This retrospective study included adults diagnosed between 2017-2021 with stage IIIA NSCLC, and who started treatment within 6 months of diagnosis, from ConcertAI’s Patient360TM NSCLC product - a large, representative, de-identified oncology database of comprehensive human-curated real world data sourced from US academic and community electronic health records (EHRs) with full data provenance. Patients treated with targeted therapy, upstaged prior to treatment start, with T3/N2 disease, or with a second primary cancer within one year of diagnosis were excluded. Progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS) were evaluated using Kaplan Meier and Cox proportional hazard methods. Baseline differences were balanced using propensity score-derived inverse probability treatment weighting (IPTW). Results: Of 2223 eligible patients, 562 (25%) received S+C and 720 (32%) received DCI. Use of guideline-recommended treatment varied among community practices (Figure 1). [Formula presented] Compared to patients treated with DCI, patients treated with S+C had 24% lower hazard of progression and 35% lower hazard of overall mortality after controlling for baseline differences. This result was consistent for N2 and N0/N1 disease (Table 1). [Formula presented] Conclusions: Use of NCCN recommended therapies for stage IIIA NSCLC varies considerably among US oncology practices, which could reflect preference-guided rather than evidence-guided decision-making. Of the top patient therapies, surgery with chemotherapy is associated with improved survival and progression-free survival compared to chemoradiation with consolidation immunotherapy. Keywords: Stage IIIA, Surgery, Immunotherapy
