N2  - Introduction: In the phase 3 AEGEAN trial, perioperative durvalumab + neoadjuvant chemotherapy, versus neoadjuvant chemotherapy alone, significantly improved event-free survival (EFS) and pathological complete response (pCR), with manageable safety, in patients with resectable NSCLC (R-NSCLC). However, patients with known EGFR/ALK aberrations were excluded from efficacy analyses in the modified intent-to-treat (mITT) population. We report exploratory analyses of patients with EGFR-mutated (EGFRm) R-NSCLC in AEGEAN. Methods: AEGEAN is a placebo-controlled study of patients aged ≥18 years with treatment-naïve R-NSCLC (stage II-IIIB[N2]; AJCC 8th ed) and ECOG PS 0/1. Patients were randomized (1:1) to receive neoadjuvant platinum-based chemotherapy plus durvalumab or placebo IV (Q3W for 4 cycles) prior to surgery, followed by durvalumab or placebo IV (Q4W up to 12 cycles), respectively, after surgery. Randomization was stratified by disease stage (II vs III) and PD-L1 expression (<1% vs ≥1%; Ventana SP263). EGFR mutation status was assessed centrally. The primary endpoints were EFS (RECIST v1.1), per BICR, and pCR, evaluated centrally, in the mITT population. Secondary endpoints included major pathological response (MPR), evaluated centrally, in the mITT population, and safety, assessed in patients with ≥1 study treatment dose. Outcomes were evaluated in the EGFRm patient subgroup. Results: Of 802 patients randomized to the ITT population, 51 had EGFR mutations (durvalumab arm, n=26; placebo arm, n=25), all of whom received ≥1 study treatment dose. Fewer patients in the durvalumab versus placebo arm were male (30.8% vs 48.0%) or Asian (57.7% vs 84.0%), however, median age was similar (63.5 vs 64.0 years); 57.7% versus 64.0% had Stage III disease, 38.5% versus 32.0% had PD-L1 expression <1%, and 11.5% versus 4.0% had squamous histology. 84.6% and 92.0% in the durvalumab and placebo arms, respectively, received 4 cycles of chemotherapy; 73.1% and 80.0% completed surgery. As of Nov 10, 2022 (DCO), median EFS follow-up in censored patients was 16.6 months. In the EGFRm subgroup, the unstratified EFS HR was 0.86 (95% CI, 0.35-2.19; vs mITT: stratified HR, 0.68; 95% CI, 0.53-0.88; P=0.003902). pCR and MPR, with respective outcomes for the mITT population, are shown (Table 1). Maximum grade 3/4 AEs occurred in 42.3% (durvalumab arm) versus 40.0% (placebo arm) of patients. Conclusions: Among patients with EGFRm R-NSCLC, there is no clear evidence of clinical benefit in the durvalumab versus placebo arm. However, these findings should be interpreted with caution due to the small number of patients and wide confidence intervals. Keywords: Durvalumab, perioperative, EGFR [Formula presented]
