N2  - Introduction: In patients with locally-advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the invasion of major structures, such as the mediastinum and large blood vessels of the heart, tends to be refractory and unresectable. Multi-Disciplinary Treatment (MDT) would be generally applied for the optimal clinical decision. It is still not clear whether and how much benefits that the surgery-based strategy might provide to patients. Methods: This retrospective cohort study was conducted by using the population-based Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results database (SEER) from 2010 to 2019, and we identified T4N0-3M0 NSCLC patients with the central structure invasion, under the AJCC 7/8 criteria. Based on the timeline for the development of precision medicine, three eras were divided: 2010-2015 for the targeted drugs proved by FDA; 2016-2017 for the initial approval of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs); 2018-2019 for the updated ICIs (Durvalumab after concurrent chemo-radiotherapy and neoadjuvant chemo-immune therapy). The benefits of surgery-/non-surgery based treatment were evaluated by Kaplan-Meier estimators and Cox proportional hazard models. Data were collected and analyzed from 1st February to 31st March, 2023. All statistical tests were two-sided with a significance threshold of p < 0.05. Results: A total of 20,150 patients were identified, including 2,955 patients who underwent surgery-based treatment. The median overall survival (mOS) has significantly increased with the development of precision medicine: 14.0 months for 2010-2015; 16.0 months for 2017-2018; 22.0 months for 2018-2019. Surgery-based treatment obviously prolonged the mOS (30.0 months), followed by: chemoradiation (16.0), chemotherapy alone (10.0) and radiation alone (6.0). Moreover, Cox survival analysis of each time period favored the modality based on surgery both on univariate and multivariate analysis, especially in the most recently era (HR=0.40, 95% CI 0.27-0.59, p < 0.001). Patients who were aged ≥ 65 years, male, stage N2-3 and pathologically diagnosed with squamous cell lung cancer had the shortened OS under multivariate analysis (p < 0.001, respectively). In the era of 2018-2019, the Kaplan-Meier survival curves showed a clear trend toward increased survival benefit in patients receiving chemotherapy alone and in male patients respectively, although mOS has not yet been reached. Conclusions: With the development of precision medicine for NSCLC, personalized treatment strategy was optimized depending on the more effective corporations of MDT, consequently prolonged the patients’ survival. Combing the peri-operation treatments in a radical intend, the surgery-based strategy showed the superior benefits among these patients with major structure invasion. [Formula presented] Keywords: Non–small cell lung cancer, Surgery, Precision Medicine
