N2  - Introduction: CANOPY-1 (NCT03631199) was a Phase III study evaluating the efficacy and safety of pembrolizumab plus platinum-based doublet chemotherapy combined with canakinumab or placebo as first-line therapy for patients with advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC). The primary endpoints of progression-free survival (PFS) and overall survival (OS) were not met, and no significant difference in clinical response was observed across predefined biomarker subgroups (including PD-L1, TMB, CRP, and IL-6). CD8+ tumor-infiltrating lymphocytes play a pivotal role in antitumor immune responses and high infiltration is associated with improved clinical outcomes when combined with immune checkpoint inhibitor (ICI) therapy. Here, we present exploratory analyses to evaluate the prognostic role of CD8+ in patients treated with ICI and chemotherapy as well as the impact of canakinumab in subgroups of patients defined by their CD8 count in tumor samples. Methods: A CD8/panCK dual immunohistochemistry assay was used to quantify both the total CD8 count and the spatial localization of CD8+ cells in the tumor versus the stroma compartment based on a predefined algorithm, leading to classification of baseline tumor samples according to three phenotypes: immune desert, excluded, and inflamed. Two analyses of CD8 subgroups were performed using samples from 500 out of the 643 patients enrolled in CANOPY-1: total CD8 count in tumor samples (low versus high based on the median cut-off) and T-cell phenotype (immune desert versus excluded versus inflamed). Additionally, whole-transcriptome data generated by RNAseq of baseline tumor samples were available for 298 patients. Descriptive statistical comparisons and a Cox model were used to assess the prognostic and predictive roles of biomarkers. Listed p-values are nominal. Results: High T-cell infiltration status (patients with a high total CD8 count or inflamed phenotype) was associated with improved OS and PFS, regardless of treatment arm. In patients with a low total CD8 count, canakinumab treatment showed a trend towards improved median OS (17.1 versus 12.9 months; HR 0.722; 95% CI 0.515‒1.010; p=0.134) and improved median PFS (6.7 versus 4.9 months; HR 0.531; 95% CI 0.363‒0.778; p<0.001) compared with placebo. In patients with an immune desert T-cell phenotype, median PFS improved compared with placebo (6.9 versus 5.0 months; HR 0.469; 95% CI 0.216‒1.020; p=0.009), while median OS was not reached in the canakinumab arm compared with 10.4 months in the placebo arm (HR 0.583; 95% CI 0.311‒1.090; p=0.141). There were no clinically relevant imbalances in baseline characteristics between the treatment groups in the subgroups defined by total CD8 count and T-cell phenotype. Transcriptome analysis revealed that CD8-low/immune desert subgroups had higher suppressive myeloid tumor microenvironment features. Conclusions: The results of predefined exploratory biomarker analyses from CANOPY-1 indicated a positive trend towards treatment benefit with canakinumab in patients with low T-cell infiltration (low total count or immune desert phenotype). Preclinical studies and the neoadjuvant CANOPY-N trial recently showed that canakinumab combined with ICI could decrease immunosuppressive features and activate a CD8-driven antitumor response (Mok TSK, et al. ESMO Asia 2022), providing potential rationale for the findings from CANOPY-1 subgroup analyses. Further clinical validation is warranted. Keywords: NSCLC, IL-1β, biomarkers
