N2  - Introduction: Peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) potentially served as an idea liquid biopsy approach to evaluate immune activation status and treatment response after administration of immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs). In this study, gene expression profile in PBMCs at baseline and during the dynamic course of immunotherapy were extensively analyzed, and the relationship between dynamic changes in patients' PBMCs and response to neoadjuvant immunotherapy were comprehensively investigated. Methods: A total of 43 patients with stage IIB-IIIB NSCLC received neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy from August 2019 to July 2021 were enrolled. 10ml peripheral blood were collected at baseline (PB0), 7 days after neoadjuvant therapy (PB1), 21 days after neoadjuvant therapy (PB2) and 42 days after neoadjuvant therapy (PB3). PBMC were isolated and RNA was extracted subsequently subjected to a targeted RNA sequencing panel covering 2660 genes. (Amoy Diagnostics, Xiamen, China). Immune cell component was predicted via established deconvolution models (CIBERSORT, DNN-DAISM, MCP-Counter etc.) and single sample gene set enrichment analysis (ssGSEA) using pre-defined gene sets of immune cell types. Differential gene expression was analyzed through Mann-Whitney U test. Results: MHC I antigen presentation pathway and CD8+ T cell differentiation were both upregulated (p<0.05) in one week after 1st course of chemo-immunotherapy (PB1). Especially, in PBMCs of MPR patients at PB1, HLA-A, HLA-DQA1 and naïve CD8 T cell were significantly enriched. In PB2, antigen presenting process was continuously activated presented as upregulation of CIITA and sustained HLA-B level in MPR, but downregulation of those ones in non-MPR patients. Furthermore, after 2 cycles of chemo-immunotherapy, Effector/cytotoxic CD8+ cells were over-presented in MPR group, together with a rapid decline of NK cell and its marker, CD160 in non-MPR patients. In addition, PBMCs from MPR patients exhibited stable expression level of pro-inflammatory CCL4, XCL2, as well as up-regulated CCL23 and IL10. While expression of these molecules continued to decrease in PBMCs from non-MPR patients. Conclusions: This pilot study suggests rapid activation of HLA-I/II molecules followed by transition of CD8+ cells from naïve to cytotoxic T cells as well as activation of NK cells during first two treatment courses putatively indicated the therapeutic efficacy and responsive mechanism of immunotherapy. Keywords: HLA, PBMC, neoadjuvant therapy
