N2  - Introduction: Locally advanced non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) harboring rare mutations represents a challenging clinical scenario, for which a standard therapeutic approach has yet to be established. However, in the presence of programmed death-ligand 1 (PD-L1) expression, neoadjuvant immunotherapy may offer promising outcomes by inducing favorable pathological responses. Nonetheless, the response patterns and immunological profile of patients with NSCLC and rare mutations in the context of immunotherapy treatment modalities remain inadequately elucidated. Methods: In this study, we performed single-cell RNA sequencing on 9 NSCLC specimens bearing rare oncogenic drivers, including ERBB2 p.Y772_A775dup, KIF5B-RET fusions, and BRAF V600E, which were receiving neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy and local consolidation therapy. Histological sections were reviewed to evaluate pathological response. Results: We identified and annotated a total of 48,188 cells across eight major cell subsets of immune, tumor, and stromal cells. Similar to the observations in previous studies, the major pathological response (MPR) group had a higher level of infiltrating T cells and natural killer (NK) cells than the non-MPR group, while the distribution of cell types varied among different rare driver mutations. The tumor harboring BRAF V600E mutation with the major pathological response (MPR) showed enrichment of IGHG+ plasma cells. In contrast, myeloid cells, especially macrophages, have a broad range of phenotypes enriched for profibrotic genes (e.g., CHI3L1, FABP4, and SPP1) in BRAF V600E with non-MPR. In patients carrying ERBB2 mutations, the patient with 20% residual tumor demonstrated a higher expression of TNFSF9+, HSPA1B+, and RPL41+ memory B cell, CXCL13+ CD4, and HSPA1A+ CD4 T cell than patient with 60% residual tumor. Furthermore, in comparison to the patient with 60% residual tumor, the T cell receptor signaling pathway was upregulated in the patient with 20% residual tumor. FGFBP2+ NK cells were enriched in the tumor with KIF5B-RET fusions that presented a pathological complete response. Conclusions: This study revealed a unique immune landscape at the single-cell level in rare-mutant NSCLC undergoing neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy. These findings may have implications for the development of personalized therapeutic strategies for patients with rare mutations in locally advanced NSCLC. [Formula presented] Keywords: non-small cell lung cancer, rare mutations, immunotherapy
