N2  - Introduction: Both the neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy and neoadjuvant chemotherapy has improved poor prognosis to vary degrees in patients with resectable NSCLC. However, efficacy difference between two neoadjuvant approaches still needs more evidence. Therefore, we conducted a retrospective study from real world to compare efficacy of two different neoadjuvant strategies, and determined factor related to prognosis. Methods: Patients with NSCLC received both neoadjuvant therapy and resection were collected. Radiological evaluations before and after neoadjuvant therapy were conducted according to RECIST criteria. All pathological slices of surgical specimen were evaluated to determine pathological response. Prognosis analysis was performed with Kaplan-Meier estimator and Cox proportional hazards regression model. Results: 57 patients received neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy and 46 patients received neoadjuvant chemotherapy were enrolled. Neoadjuvant immunotherapy plus chemotherapy provided a OR (objective response) rate at 75%, MPR (major pathological response) rate at 63% and cPR (pathological complete response) rate at 37%, higher than rate of OR (52%), MPR (22%) and cPR (9%) achieved in neoadjuvant chemotherapy group. More favorable RFS (recurrence-free survival) and OS (overall survival) was observed in neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy group. And there was strong correlation between MPR status and RFS in patients with neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy. Conclusions: Our research indicated that neoadjuvant immunotherapy plus chemotherapy was more superior in producing high rate of OR, MPR, cPR and offering better prognosis. Neoadjuvant immunotherapy combined with chemotherapy deserves recommendation for resectable NSCLC. Additionally, MPR was significant predictor for recurrence-free survival in patients treated with neoadjuvant immunotherapy plus chemotherapy. [Formula presented] [Formula presented] Keywords: Neoadjuvant, Immunotherapy, Major pathological response (MPR)
