N2  - Introduction: The previous retrospective study(2022WCLCEP02.04-010) showed promising MPR and tolerability of neoadjuvant tislelizumab combined with chemotherapy in resectable stage IIIA-B NSCLC, meanwhile did not impact the feasibility of surgery. This study aimed to evaluate long-term EFS benefit in stage IIB-III NSCLC in a real-world setting. Methods: We retrospectively collected medical data of patients(pts) who received neoadjuvant tislelizumab combined with chemotherapy and adjuvant tislelizumab therapy between 2020-2022 at our center. The primary endpoint was 2-year event free survival (2-year EFS) rate; secondary endpoints included major pathological response (MPR), pathological complete response(pCR), object response rate (ORR), surgical outcomes, and safety etc. Results: There were 23 patients eligible for inclusion. 20 pts received 3-4 cycles neoadjuvant therapy. 22 pts received surgery successfully and the median surgery interval were 33.5 (range 21-50) days. 18 pts received adjuvant tislelizumab therapy with median 4(range 1-23) cycles(Q3W) and the median adjuvant therapy interval were 30 (range 25-90) days. As of data cut-off on March 30,2023, follow-up with median 21 (range 4-35) months, 2-years EFS rate was 76.2% (95%CI:42.7-91.7) and median EFS did not arrive. The ORR was 85% (95%CI:62.1-96.8) and CR rate was 5% (95%CI:0.12-24.8); The surgery rate was 95.65% (95%CI:78.05-99.89) and R0 resection rate was 100%. The pathological down-staging rate was 90.9% (95%CI:70.8-98.9). The MPR was 72.7% (95%CI:49.8-89.3)and pCR was 36.4%(95%CI:17.2-51.3). Immune related AEs (n, %, grade) included pruritus (n=1,4.3%, grade1), dermatitis (n=1,4.3%, grade1), myelosuppression (n=1,4.3%, grade3). Conclusions: Neoadjuvant and adjuvant tislelizumab combined with chemotherapy showed long-term EFS benefit and tolerable adverse events in stage IIB-III NSCLC in real word. [Formula presented] Keywords: Long-term efficacy and safety, neoadjuvant/adjuvant therapy, Tislelizumab plus chemotherapy
