N2  - Introduction: Patients with stage IIIA/IIIB non-small cell lung cancers (NSCLC) are potentially curable, however 5-year survival rate remains disappointing of 40%. The median overall survival(OS) of stage III resectable NSCLC was only 31months, and 22 months for N2. Antibodies that block programmed death 1 (PD-1) protein combined with chemotherapy improves survival in patients with resectable IB-IIIA NSCLC. Sintilimab, a highly selective, fully human monoclonal antibody (mAb), blocks the interaction of PD-1 and its ligands and has demonstrated clinical benefit in various clinical studies. Therefore, for those patients with potentially resectable IIIA/IIIB NSCLC who definitely need neoadjuvant therapy, it is still a question to be explored whether immunochemotherapy combined with chemotherapy can improve the pathological response rate, the disease-free survival(DFS) and OS. This study assessed the effects of the addition of Sintilimab to traditionnal neoadjuvant chemotherapy in potentially resecrable IIIA/IIIB NSCLC, aims to observe whether Sintilimab in combination with chemotherapy can be a new choice to improve survival rate and provide more possibilities of surgical resection for IIIA/IIIB NSCLC. Methods: This prospective, single-center, single-arm, phase 2 clinical trial (registration number: NCT04326153) included treatment-naive patients with potentially resectable NSCLC (stage IIIA/IIIB) who received sintilimab plus nab-paclitaxel and carboplatin for two to three cycles before systematic nodal dissection 30 to 60 days after neoadjuvant treatment. After surgery, patients needed to complete two cycles of adjuvant chemoimmunotherapy (sintilimab + nab-paclitaxel + carboplatin). The primary endpoint was disease-free survival rate at 24 months, whereas secondary endpoints included major pathological response (MPR) and pathologic complete response (pCR) rates, the overall survival, objective response rate (ORR), and adverse effects. Results: Between December 2019 and November 2021, 30 patients were enrolled, of whom 20 were underwent surgery. Nine of the other ten patients who did not underwent surgery achieved PR, but unfortunately missed the opportunity for surgery due to the COVID19. The ORR and DCR were 67.7% and 96.7%, respectively. R0 resection was achieved in all surgical cases. Nine (45%) patients had a pCR and 13 (65%) patients had a MPR. At the time of data cutoff (March 2023), median follow-up time was 30.6 months. The median DFS and OS were not reached. The current two years DFS rate was 74.6%. Severe (grade 3-4) TRAE included myocardial enzyme(1of 30, 3.3%), interstitial pneumonia leukopenia (1 of 30, 3.3%), and bronchopleural fistula(1of 30, 3.3%). Conclusions: Neoadjuvant Sintilimab plus chemotherapy is a new choice to provide more opportunities of surgical resection and improve the DFS and OS for IIIA/IIIB NSCLC. Keywords: adjuvant, immunochemotherapy, non-small cell lung cancer
