N2  - Introduction: Combined chemo-immune therapy demonstrates remarkable therapeutic responsiveness at neoadjuvant setting in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), with responding mechanism remains not fully understood. Methods: We characterized the transcriptomes of ∼140,000 single cells and the T-cell receptor (TCR) immune repertoire of ∼50,000 single immune cells derived from 18 paired pre-treatment and post-treatment lung tumor samples from 9 IIB-IIIB NSCLC patients who received combined neoadjuvant PD-1 inhibitor with chemotherapy. Among those, pathological responses were evaluated as non-major pathologic response (non-MPR, n=3), MPR (n=3), and pathologic complete response (PCR, n=3). Results: Responsive tumors (tumors with MPR and PCR) held the higher proportion of T cells than non-MPR tumors, both at baseline and at post-treatment. Cluster-based proportions exhibited increased proportion of CD8+ T clusters in responsive tumors and, conversely, increased proportion of Tregs in non-MPR tumors. We next investigated TCR repertoire dynamics. Responsive tumors possessed richer clonal abundance than non-MPR tumors. Responsive tumors enriched highly-expanded TCR clones in CD8+ effector memory T cells (CD8+ EM). We then characterized TCR clones according to copy number changes from pre- to post-treatment. CD8+ EM clones were mainly characterized as expanded, including in situ persistently expansion from pre- to post-treatment, and novel clonal expansion that not existed prior to treatment. These CD8+ EM expanded clones exhibited enhanced functions in T cell activation, cellular killing and immune response to tumor. On the other hand, non-MPR tumors were dominant by enriched TCR clones in Tregs. These Tregs clones were mainly novel clones at post-treatment. We characterized Treg functions via pathway scoring and found that SMAD and IL-2 pathway were functionally activated both in highly-expanded Treg clones at post-treatment and PDCD1-positive Treg clones at pre-treatment. Conclusions: In general, functionally enhanced CD8+ T cells and Tregs, respectively, positively and negatively contributed to neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy in NSCLC. Our study provided first insight into responding mechanism behind neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy using paired tissuee specimens, implicating chemoimmunotherapy induced differential clonal patterns of CD8+ effective T cells and Tregs as the trigger to distinct responsiveness. Keywords: Neoadjuvant chemoimmunotherapy efficacy, Tumor microenvironment, Single-cell sequencing
