N2  - Introduction: The global burden of disease study revealed that lung cancer is a leading cause of death worldwide. The only patients with a prospect of cure are early non-small cell lung cancer (eNSCLC) amenable to surgery. However, many experience poor survival from recurrence and aggressive tumor microenvironment, which includes patient immune expression patterns undiagnosed with current clinical technology. Peri-operative immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) provide promise to improve survival in eNSCLC. However, data on optimal immunohistohemistry (IHC) approach to select patients who will benefit from ICIs is needed. We hypothesize that immune cell group classification using hyperspectral multiplex IHC (mICH) can be used to identify eNCSLC at risk of recurrence, who may derive clinical benefit from ICIs. Methods: Consecutive patients with confirmed eNSCLC were enrolled from thoracic surgery clinic. Following surgery, histopathologic synoptic reporting was conducted. Tumor tissue was subject to NGS and hyperspectral mIHC from slides cut from the core FFPE (Figure 1.) Antibodies chosen were selected based upon markers that have of direct therapeutic relevance via the T cell biological mechanism in anti-PD1/PDL1 immune therapy. Results: 16 consecutive cases underwent curative intent resection for eNSCLC. Mean(SD) age 69 (9), and 14 (87.5%) female. Never smokers comprised 8 (50%), while former or current smokers 8 (50%). Predominant histopathology was adenocarcinoma 15 (94%), and stage IA 9 (56%). 5 (31%) eNSCLC suffered recurrent cancer at 2 years despite complete resection. Hyperspectral mIHC revealed significantly higher PDL1+ total cell counts in those cases of recurrent eNSCLC versus non-recurrent (3767 vs. 390; p=0.029 (Table 1). Conclusions: The hyperspectral mIHC preliminary results show PDL1+ cell counts to be significantly higher in recurrent eNSCLC after complete resection. Results could be used to refine which NSCLC patients may benefit from perioperative ICIs, and preliminary findings validated in a larger ongoing study that includes additional molecular markers. [Formula presented] [Formula presented]
