N2  - Introduction: For patients (pts) with resectable stage III(N2) non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the single-arm SAKK 16/14 trial suggested significant improvements in pathological complete response (pCR), major pathological remission (MPR), and event-free survival (EFS) with the addition of perioperative durvalumab to standard neoadjuvant chemotherapy (ChT) with cisplatin and docetaxel. The ongoing SAKK 16/18 trial is evaluating whether immune-modulatory radiotherapy (RT) administered concurrently with neoadjuvant durvalumab can further improve efficacy outcomes. Methods: In this non-comparative randomized phase II trial, pts with resectable stage IIIA-B(N2) NSCLC (cT1-3 or T4 due to size N2 M0) are randomized to three cycles of cisplatin 100mg/m2 and docetaxel 85mg/m2 followed by one dose of durvalumab (1500mg) concomitantly with one of three RT regimens (20x2Gy, 5x5Gy, 3x8Gy) to the primary tumor only. Tumor resection takes place 4-6 weeks after RT start and is followed by adjuvant durvalumab for 1 year. The primary endpoint is 1-year EFS. We report on the secondary endpoints of the interim analysis focusing on the impact of neoadjuvant multimodality treatment on surgical outcomes including resectability and perioperative complications. Results: At the cut-off date of October 8, 2022, 25 pts had reached postoperative day 30, and a total of 31 pts were included in the safety analysis. Median age of pts was 65 years. Adenocarcinoma was the predominant histology (18 pts; 58%). Stage IIIA was evident in 16 (52%) and stage IIIB in 15 pts (48%). 30 pts (97%) had at least one treatment-related adverse event (TRAE). 88% of all TRAEs were attributable to neoadjuvant ChT (16% G3-4), 4% each to neoadjuvant durvalumab (1% G3) and RT (1% G4), and 8% to surgery (35% G3-4). 81% of pts (n=25) underwent surgery. The reasons for non-resection were death (COVID, n=1), disease progression (n=3), and non-resectable tumor (n=2). A minimally invasive approach was possible in 44%. Systematic radical lymph node dissection was performed in 88%. R0 resection was achieved in 96% of pts. 44% of the resected patients had at least one AE related to surgery with highest grade 2 in 5 pts (20%), grade 3 in 5 pts (20%) and grade 4 in 1 pt (4%). The 30-day mortality was 0%. The pCR rate was 28%, and MPR (≤10% viable tumor cells) was recorded in 76% of all resected tumors. Details of surgical outcomes associated with each RT regimen will be presented at the meeting. Conclusions: The pre-planned interim safety analysis of the SAKK 16/18 trial confirmed that surgery post-neoadjuvant trimodality treatment was feasible and safe. The trial will continue with a planned total number of 90 patients (30 per RT arm). Keywords: Surgical interim analysis, Stage III(N2), Combination treatment
