N2  - Background: Differentiating cancer progression from treatment-related changes is an important aspect of follow up after treatment of brain metastases (BM). Stereotactic radiosurgery (SRS) especially when used in combination with immunotherapy (ImT) and/or tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKI) can be associated with increased potential for treatment- related changes, that can mimic progression on routine imaging. Specialized imaging such as magnetic resonance (MR) spectroscopy and perfusion studies are valuable tools to potentially differentiate progression from treatment-related changes (Weinberg BD, et al Radiol Clin North Am 2021). Objectives: We queried the rate of MRI spectroscopy/perfusion after SRS for brain metastases and within this subset we analyzed ImT/TKI use, imaging result accuracy, and subsequent interventions. Methods: Patients age>/= 18 who underwent SRS for BM at Baylor Scott and White (BSW) between Jan 2017-Jan 2022 were analyzed using archived information from ARIA/Eclipse and EPIC EMR to collect demographic and, clinical data, radiation courses, MR spectroscopy/ perfusion studies, and subsequent interventions. Results: Our analysis included 161 patients who received SRS with a total of 231 radiation courses. 16 patients (10%) underwent>/= 1 MR spectroscopy/perfusion study; all were treated for BM with following primaries: 8 NSCLC, 3 melanoma, 2 RCC, 2 breast, 1 esophageal. Of these, further analysis was completed for 10 patients (Table 1); excluded patients had inadequate follow up (moved or died not long after MR), or ambiguous/obscure data (ex=several lesions with multiple rounds of SRS and MRs). The subset consisted of 50% males, and 100% had ImT or TKI use during the relevant course: 3 with neoadjuvant [N], concurrent [C], and adjuvant [A] use; 4 with C and A use; and 3 with A use. Of the 7 patients (70%) who had findings consistent with progressive disease on MR, 57% (N =4) were taken for surgical resection with 50% of pathology showing radiation necrosis (RN) only, indicating discordance between imaging and pathology. Remaining 30% of patients with MR findings indicating progression underwent no further local therapy (N=2) or repeat radiation was attempted. The patients with MRs that were non-specific (ruled negative for progression) or read as pseudo-progression/radiation necrosis, had no further interventions with no evidence of progression of these lesions on follow up. Conclusions: Findings suggestive of progression even after specialized imaging such as MR spectroscopy/perfusion must be both interpreted and acted upon cautiously, especially in the setting of more frequent use of SRS and ImT/TKI, as our retrospective study reveals 50% discordance between imaging and pathology results.
