N2  - Aim/background: While definitive chemoradiation (CTRT) remains the standard of care in patients with locally advanced, unresectable stage III NSCLC, patients with contralateral mediastinal and cervical are a poor risk group, due to higher rates of loco-regional and distant failures. Materials and methods: We retrospectively analyzed patients with histologically proven stage III NSCLC and N3 disease, who were treated with CTRT, between April 2014 and March 2022 at our institute. Treatment plan was discussed in a multi-disciplinary tumour board and patients were followed up as per institution policy. Statistical analysis was done using IBM SPSS Statistics (Version 26.0) Results: Of the total 712 NSCLC patients treated with radical radiation therapy, 42 patients (6%) were eligible (neck node-43%; contralateral mediastinal or hilar node-57%); mean age of the population was 63 years (range – 32 to 80 years); 86% were men. Neoadjuvant, concurrent and adjuvant chemotherapy were administered in 14%, 86% and 12% respectively. Average GTVp, GTVn and PTV volume were 89cc, 20.9cc and 404cc respectively; mean lung dose, V20Gy and V5Gy were 9Gy, 20% and 58% respectively; mean heart dose, V30Gy and V40Gy were 13Gy, 12% and 6% respectively. At a median follow-up of 17 months, 21 patients had progressed and 29 patients (69%) remain alive. Five patients (12%) developed grade 3 RT induced pneumonitis. Median PFS was 14 months; 1-yr, 2-yr PFS and OS rates were 78%, 63% and 92%, 70% respectively. Univariate analysis showed significant PFS benefit in patients who received either NACT or adjuvant chemotherapy. Conclusion: In a group of carefully selected patients with N3 disease, survival outcomes were comparable to published real-world stage III N2 disease data. Modern RT techniques have made treating complex and challenging treatment volumes possible without undue increase in acute and chronic toxicities. Benefit of adjuvant immunotherapy in this high-risk subgroup currently remains uncharted. Disclosure: No significant relationships.
