N2  - Introduction: In the open-label, phase 3 CheckMate 816 study (NCT02998528), neoadjuvant NIVO+chemo vs chemo alone demonstrated statistically significant and clinically meaningful improvement in pathologic complete response (pCR) and EFS, without increasing post-surgical complications, in patients with resectable NSCLC. Here, we report additional surgical outcomes. Methods: Patients with newly diagnosed, stage IB (≥4 cm) to IIIA NSCLC and no known sensitizing EGFR/ALK alterations were randomized 1:1 to 3 cycles of neoadjuvant NIVO 360 mg + chemo Q3W or chemo Q3W, followed by surgery ≤6 weeks post-treatment. Primary endpoints were pCR and EFS. In these post-hoc analyses, EFS was evaluated by surgical approach, extent of resection, and completeness of resection. Results: At database lock (20-October-2021), median follow-up was 29.5 months. Of 358 patients randomized to NIVO+chemo (n=179) or chemo (n=179), 149 (83%) and 135 (75%) received definitive surgery. In patients who underwent surgery, EFS improvement was seen with NIVO+chemo vs chemo alone (Table 1), regardless of surgical approach (hazard ratio [HR]: 0.46 [minimally invasive surgery]; 0.67 [thoracotomy]), extent of resection (HR: 0.58 [lobectomy]; not calculated [pneumonectomy]), or completeness of resection (HR: 0.59 [R0 resection]; not calculated [R1 resection]); HR was not calculated in some subgroups owing to limited sample size or number of EFS events. Notably, median EFS with NIVO+chemo was not reached for most subgroups. Two-year NIVO+chemo EFS rates (95% CI) were: 81% (66-90) [minimally invasive surgery]; 69% (58-78) [thoracotomy]; 70% (60-78) [lobectomy]; 76% (52-90) [pneumonectomy]; and 72% (62-79) [R0 resection]. Baseline characteristics for all patients who received surgery as well as updated results from a database lock of 20-October-2022 will be presented. [Figure presented] Conclusions: NIVO+chemo did not impede surgical feasibility in CheckMate 816 and EFS benefit was observed in patients receiving NIVO+chemo vs chemo. These results further support neoadjuvant NIVO+chemo as a treatment option for patients with resectable NSCLC. Disclosure: JS: Consulting: AZ, Merck, Roche, BMS, Novartis, Chemocentryx, Amgen, Protalix Biotherapeutics, Xenetic Bio sciences, Regeneron; Grant: AZ, Merck, Roche, CLS Therapeutics, Protalix Biotherapeutics; Clinical Trial Role: BMS, Novartis, Roche, Merck, AZ
