N2  - Introduction: Recent trials have demonstrated promising results of neoadjuvant and adjuvant chemoimmunotherapy combinations in addition to surgical resection in patients with locally advanced non-small cell lung cancer(NSCLC). However in practice, rates of systemic therapy utilization have historically been lower than expected. This is particularly applicable to patients undergoing pneumonectomy, in which there is typically a significant disease burden but where the physiological insult of surgery may preclude adjuvant therapy. We therefore explored the utilization of systemic therapy for patients undergoing pneumonectomy. Methods: We queried the National Cancer Database(NCDB), including all patients who underwent pneumonectomy for NSCLC(2004-2017) and stratified the cohort based upon neoadjuvant therapy or upfront surgery with or without adjuvant therapy. We utilized logistic regression to identify factors associated with receipt of neoadjuvant systemic therapy and with any systemic therapy in a second model. Lastly, the survival benefit of systemic therapy was quantified with Cox regression. Results: A total of 2,619 patients were included. Among these, 314(12%) received neoadjuvant therapy. Multivariable analysis revealed that age<65(OR:1.53,95%CI 1.10-2.11), Asian ethnicity(OR:2.68,95%CI 1.37-5.23), treatment at a high volume center (OR:1.39,95%CI 1.06-1.81), and private insurance(OR:1.42,95%CI 1.05-1.94) associated with use of neoadjuvant strategy. Among patients not receiving neoadjuvant, only 1,116(48.%) received adjuvant therapy. On a separate multivariable model age <65(OR:1.95,95%CI 1.61-2.38), comorbidity index≤1 (OR:1.66,95%CI 1.29-2.16), private insurance (OR:1.47,95%CI 1.20-1.80), and surgery after 2015(OR:1.43,95%CI 1.14-1.80) were associated with the use of systemic treatment. By Cox Regression, receipt of systemic therapy was associated with markedly better survival than surgery alone (HR:0.54,95%CI 0.49-0.60). Conclusion: A high proportion of patients undergoing pneumonectomy do not receive any systemic therapy. Both patient and socioeconomic factors are associated with the receipt of systemic therapy. Given the survival benefit associated with systemic therapy compared to surgery alone, emphasis should be placed on multimodal treatment strategies, perhaps with greater consideration given to neoadjuvant approaches. [Formula presented]
