N2  - Surgery is the main treatment in stage I-IIIA NSCLC. Several phase 3 randomized adjuvant and neoadjuvant trials with platinum-based chemotherapy were performed in these stages. These trials demonstrated a significant 5 % benefit in 5y-survival in favour of (neo)-adjuvant chemotherapy in stage II and IIIA. This benefit was confirmed in two meta-analyses of individual participant data. Conversely for stages I the benefit of chemotherapy was suggested only for tumors > 4 cm. Lung-ART/IFCT-0503 trial did show the lack of benefit for adjuvant radiotherapy in fully-resected N2 patients. The peri-operative impact of targeted treatments in NSCLC with molecular alterations is actually suggested by one study in patients with EGFR mutations. Finally, the checkmate 816 trial suggested that neoadjuvant immune checkpoint inhibitors in association to standard chemotherapy provide a significant benefit in event-free survival, although overall survival data are still awaited. 1877-1203/© 2022 SPLF. Published by Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.
