N2  - Background: The treatment algorithm of stage III NSCLC involves a multimodality approach including surgery (S) or definitive chemo-radiotherapy (CRT). In 2018, the PACIFIC trial established consolidation durvalumab (D) as standard of care after concurrent CRT (cCRT) in PD-L1 >=1% patients (pts). Aim of this study is to evaluate treatment evolution after 2018 and estimate outcomes in different treatment cohorts. Methods: We retrospectively collected data of consecutive stage III NSCLC pts, who referred to Veneto Institute of Oncology from 2012 to 2021. Relapse-free survival (RFS) was calculated as the time between diagnosis and relapse or death; overall survival (OS) as the time between diagnosis and death. Results: The study population included 232 pts: 174 (75%) received surgical multimodality treatment, 58 (25%) underwent CRT followed or not by D; among them, 31 (53.4%) received cCRT. Before 2018, pts receiving neoadjuvant therapy (NT) among all treatments accounted for 50%, after 2018 the percentage dropped to 32%; pts undergoing CRT increased from 15% to 38%, and those undergoing cCRT grew from 20% to 71%. At data cut-off (September 2021) the median follow-up was 26 months (mo). Median RFS (mRFS) was 18.7 mo (95% CI: 15.97–21.42) and median OS (mOS) was 39.9 mo (95% CI:29.47-50.33). The 2- and 5-year survival rates were 68% and 34.7%, respectively. A significant independent association was found between OS and presence of driver mutations, consolidation D, objective response to CRT. After 2018, a significant difference in mRFS (p 0.028) and mOS (p 0.001) was observed between pts undergoing S or CRT followed by D and those treated with CRT alone; in stage IIIB-C a longer mOS was observed with cCRT plus D vs NT (p 0.042). Unresectable stage III pts receiving consolidation D had a significant improvement in mRFS (HR 0.29, p 0.007) and mOS (HR 0.16, p 0.003) as compared to pts receiving CRT alone. Worse mRFS (HR 8.10, p <0.001) and mOS (HR 6.90, p <0.001) were observed in pts undergoing sequential CRT vs pts receiving cCRT. Conclusions: Our study shows that the choice of radical treatment impacts on outcome, suggesting the importance of appropriate staging at diagnosis and of a multidisciplinary approach in the decision-making process of stage III NSCLC. Legal entity responsible for the study: University of Padua. Funding: Has not received any funding. Disclosure: All authors have declared no conflicts of interest.
