N2  - Background: Recent phase I and phase II trials report the feasibilitiy of neoadjuvant C-IO prior to surgery or definitive RCT. Furthermore, the studies report high rates of pathological remission, a surrogate marker for overall survival. Methods: In two lung cancer centers, 35 patients with NSCLC and a potentially curative stage (including 12 patients with oligometastatic disease) were prospectively included if curative treatment (either definitive RCT or resection) could not be performed due to large tumor size or for functional reasons (e. g. too large radiation field or functionally inoperable for the required resection). For these patients, the multidisciplinary tumor board (MDB) recommended palliative C-IO and re-evaluation including PET-CT if indicated. After review of the MDB, patients received either definitive curative treatment or palliative treatment. Results: 14, 14, and 7 patients had a PD-L1 score (TPS) of 50%, 1–49%, and 0%, respectively. Patients completed 1–4 cycles of neoadjuvant therapy, 5 as mono immunotherapy, and 30 as chemo-immunotherapy. The primary endpoint of definitive therapy was reached by 29 patients (83%). Of these, 11 were R0-resected, 18 received curative-dose radiochemotherapy. Six patients remained palliative (2 incomplete resections (R1-R2), 4 palliative systemic therapy). The study is positive for the primary endpoint (predefined >80%). According to RECIST, 76% had complete or partial response. Of 27 patients evaluable for metabolic response (PERCIST), 7 (26%) had complete metabolic response. Of the surgical patients, 6 of 11 completely resected patients (56%) had a complete or major pathological response. After a median follow-up of 15 months, there were 13 recurrences (9 of 29 curative patients [31%], 4 of 6 palliative patients [67%]), and 5 patients had died (3 of 29 curative patients [10%], 2 of 6 palliative patients [33%]). Treatment was well tolerated with no new safety signals. Conclusions: In routine clinical practice, neo-adjuvant C-IO is feasible for patients with locally advanced or oligometastatic NSCLC and results in a high rate of curative treatment.
