N2  - Background: Pneumonitis is a common adverse event in patients treated with immunotherapy, involving treatment interruptions and potentially life-threatening situations. The aim of this study is to define a set of molecular parameters in pre-treatment blood samples of NSCLC patients treated with neo-adjuvant chemoimmunotherapy that will help to predict patients likely to develop immune-mediated pneumonitis. Methods: Treatment related adverse events and clinical variables of 46 potentially resectable stage IIIa NSCLC patients included in the NADIM clinical trial (NCT03081689) were analyzed. A semi-quantitative study of 200 plasma cytokine baseline levels was conducted in 30 patients, and peripheral blood mononuclear cells immunophenotype from 29 patients at diagnosis was characterized by flow cytometry. A comparative analysis was made with non-parametric methods between the development of immune-mediated pneumonitis of any grade during neoadjuvant treatment, and clinical and molecular variables. Results: Among pneumonitis patients 78.6% were men, with a median age of 62.5 years and 42.9% smokers. No significant differences were found between patients developing pneumonitis in terms of age, sex and smoking habits. Patients who developed pneumonitis had significant reduced cytokine levels of Cripto1, GCP2, and elevated MSPa (p-values 0.010, 0.041 and 0.026). Referring to immune cells, pneumonitis patients had a significant higher percentage of CD56+PD1- and CD8+PD1- populations, and reduced CD4+PD1+ (p-values 0.009, 0.019 and 0.032). The area under the ROC curves for pneumonitis development were 0.826, 0.758 and 0.783 for Cripto1, GCP2 and MSPa levels; and 0.831, 0.799 and 0.773 for CD56+PD1-, CD8+PD1- and CD4 +PD1+ populations.. Conclusions: In this study we describe a novel set of molecular parameters, such as cytokine levels and PBMCs populations at diagnosis, which are able to predict pneumonitis development in a context of chemoimmunotherapy. These results indicate that the study of the basal immune status from blood is a valid source of pneumonitis predictive biomarkers and supports further studies in larger cohorts.
