N2  - Introduction: The utility of tumor mutation burden (TMB) as a predictive biomarker for the benefit of an immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy has been debated intensely. Most studies have addressed the role of TMB in advanced stages, however the value of this biomarker in neoadjuvant settings has not been investigated. Here, we evaluate the association of tumor TMB with clinical outcome in resectable stage IIIA(N2) non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients undergoing neoadjuvant chemo-immunotherapy with three cycles of cisplatin/docetaxel followed by treatment with the PD-L1 antibody durvalumab. Methods: Formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissue samples from 49 patients were processed. Total DNA was extracted and used for TMB estimation using the FoundationOne CDx (F1CDx) test. Samples were processed using the F1CDx test or the Oncomine Comprehensive Assay Plus, which has shown highly concordant TMB values with F1CDx (data not shown). TMB-high was defined as >10 mutations/Mb. TMB was correlated with the primary endpoint event-free survival (EFS) at one year, overall survival (OS), major pathological response (MPR), pathological complete response (pCR), nodal clearance and nodal down-staging. Association between TMB and clinical outcome were analyzed using Logrank (Mantel-Cox) test and Mann-Whitney-Wilcox test. Analysis was performed using GraphPad Prism v9 and R version 4.0.4 (R Core Team, 2014). Results: Sixty-eight patients were enrolled and 55 patients were resected. TMB could be assessed in a total of 49 patients (10 pre-and 39 post-treatment tissue samples). Clinical parameters of these patients were not different from the overall patient population. TMB was not significantly different in patients with an EFS event after one year compared to patients with no EFS event observed (median TMB 7.5 mutations/Mb in both groups, Mann-Whitney p=.404). TMB-high patients showed no significant difference in EFS and OS (log-rank test p=.696 and .824, respectively). No significant difference of TMB based on pCR (Mann-Whitney p=.057), MPR (Mann-Whitney p=.843), nodal clearance (Mann-Whitney p=.230) and nodal down-staging (Mann-Whitney p=.170) was observed. However, in patients with pCR there was a trend towards higher TMB (median TMB 13.9 versus 6.6 mutations/Mb). Conclusions: Our results suggest that TMB is not associated with clinical outcome after neoadjuvant sequential chemo-immunotherapy with durvalumab in patients with resectable stage IIIA(N2) NSCLC.
