N2  - Currently, neoadjuvant cytototic and radiation therapies are used in standard of care surgically resectable non-small cell lung carcinoma (NSCLC) aiming to improve recurrence free and overall survivals after surgical resection. There is no prospectively conducted clinical trial addressing the role of the pathological response after neoadjuvant chemotherapy in lung cancer. A pathology-based retrospective study demonstrated that a major pathological response (MPR) defined as ≤10% viable malignant cells correlated with significantly improved disease free and overall survival in patients with stages II and III NSCLCs [1]. Importantly, pathological response did not correlate with tumor size changes determined by CT imaging [2]. This study utilized a systematic sampling with serial sections through a resected tumor followed by averaging. Unfortunately, despite the proposals by Pataer et al [1] and modified by Hellman et al [3], there are not established validated protocols and guidelines to process surgical resected tumors and assess pathological response in NSCLC after neoadjuvant therapy. Recently, with the advent of immunotherapy in lung cancer, several phase II and III clinical trials are ongoing assessing the benefit of PD-L1/PD-1 immune checkpoints blockade in the neoadjuvant setting. Several of these immunotherapy clinical trials adopted the chemotherapy MPR criteria of ≤10% viable cells as endpoint, while others are using recurrence rate. Early results in a limited number of patients has up to 45% MPR when treated with anti-PD-1 therapy and several cases showed of complete response [4]. However, as for chemotherapy, for immunotherapy there is no validated operational standard procedures (OSP) or guidelines on how best process the tumor gross specimens and to microscopically assess pathological response. There are several ongoing initiatives in the pathology community to establish these guidelines with participation of the multidisciplinary lung cancer care team. References [1] A. Pataer, N. Kalhor, A.M. Correa, M.G. Raso, J.J. Erasmus, E.S. Kim, C. Behrens, J.J. Lee, J.A. Roth, D.J. Stewart, A.A. Vaporciyan, Wistuba, II, S.G. Swisher, Histopathologic Response Criteria Predict Survival of Patients with Resected Lung Cancer After Neoadjuvant Chemotherapy, J Thorac Oncol 7(5) (2012) 825-832. [2] W.N. William, Jr., A. Pataer, N. Kalhor, A.M. Correa, D.C. Rice, Wistuba, II, J. Heymach, J.J. Lee, E.S. Kim, R. Munden, K.A. Gold, V. Papadimitrakopoulou, S.G. Swisher, J.J. Erasmus, Computed tomography RECIST assessment of histopathologic response and prediction of survival in patients with resectable non-small-cell lung cancer after neoadjuvant chemotherapy, J Thorac Oncol 8(2) (2013) 222-8. [3] M.D. Hellmann, J.E. Chaft, W.N. William, Jr., V. Rusch, K.M. Pisters, N. Kalhor, A. Pataer, W.D. Travis, S.G. Swisher, M.G. Kris, Pathological response after neoadjuvant chemotherapy in resectable non-small-cell lung cancers: proposal for the use of major pathological response as a surrogate endpoint, Lancet Oncol 15(1) (2014) e42-50. [4] P.M. Forde, J.E. Chaft, K.N. Smith, V. Anagnostou, T.R. Cottrell, M.D. Hellmann, M. Zahurak, S.C. Yang, D.R. Jones, S. Broderick, R.J. Battafarano, M.J. Velez, N. Rekhtman, Z. Olah, J. Naidoo, K.A. Marrone, F. Verde, H. Guo, J. Zhang, J.X. Caushi, H.Y. Chan, J.W. Sidhom, R.B. Scharpf, J. White, E. Gabrielson, H. Wang, G.L. Rosner, V. Rusch, J.D. Wolchok, T. Merghoub, J.M. Taube, V.E. Velculescu, S.L. Topalian, J.R. Brahmer, D.M. Pardoll, Neoadjuvant PD-1 Blockade in Resectable Lung Cancer, N Engl J Med (2018).
