N2  - In the treatment of lung cancer, especially non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), the immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) have been remarkably developed since approval of nivolumab. As a first step, several important trials reported that nivolumab, pembrolizumab and atezolizumab prolonged overall survival than docetaxel, which was a standard therapy for a long time, in NSCLC patients who became resistant to primary treatment. Subsequently, it was shown that pembrolizumab improves overall survival over platinum combination therapy, which was also long-term standard treatment of first line of NSCLC. Of note, this result was achieved by selecting a population with PDL1 TPS of 50% or more. Recently, by using ICIs in combination with platinum doublet, or by using ICIs in combination, ICIs in first-line setting has been rigorously investigated regardless of the expression of PD-L1. Meanwhile, the result of the PACIFIC was reported. In patient population with locally advanced NSCLC who did not experienced innovation for a long time, consolidation therapy by durvalumab significantly prolonged progression-free survival. The data on the overall survival of the PACIFIC will be reported in the near future, and the results have been receiving great attention. Furthermore, trials of ICIs in perioperative treatment (adjuvant and neo-adjuvant) in patients with NSCLC eligible for completely resection are underway. In this session on immunotherapy of lung cancer, findings focusing on ICIs in early and locally advanced NSCLC are reviewed.
