N2  - Background: The purpose of the study was to reveal morphological changes in tissues of NSCLC after various nPCT regimens. Methods: The study included 64 pts with stage IIb-IIIa NSCLC divided into two groups: main group - 32 pts, 2 cycles of nPCIT with recombinant hybrid protein of TNFα1; control group - 32 pts, 2 cycles of conventional PCT. CR in 3 (9,4%) and 2 (6.2%) cases, PR in 14 (43.7%) and 13 (40.6%), SD in 13 (40.6%) and 14 (43.7%), PD in 2 (6.2%) and 3 (9.4%), respectively. 29 (90.6%) pts in each group received surgery. Pts with PR had squamous cell carcinoma in 28.5% and 21.4%, adenocarcinoma in 17.9% in both groups. Tumor tissue samples of pts with PR were studied by immunohistochemistry with staining for: CD3 lymphocytes, CD20 lymphocytes,CD34 endothelial cells. An original 3-point scale of IHC expression in CD34 and CD3 cell specimens was used for the evaluation. Results: The expression of CD34 cells in the main group was weak in 69.2% and moderate in 30.8% (p = 0.044), with no strong expression; in thr control - moderate in 54.5% and strong in 45.5%. Weak CD3 expression in the main group prevailed over strong expression (53.8% vs 7.7%, p = 0.014) and in comparison with the control group (18.3%, p = 0.034). Moderate CD3 expression was observed in 38.4% and 36.4%, respectively; strong expression - 7.7% and 45.4%, respectively (p = 0.017). Thus, therapeutic pathomorphism in the nCIT group involved necrosis of up to 70-80% of tumor, with weak inflammation and no fibrosis. The endothelial vascular reaction was weak in 69.2% or moderate in 30.8%, T-lymphocytic infiltration - weak in 53.8% and moderate in 38.4%. The control group demonstrated other manifestations of therapeutic pathomorphism - up to 50% of tumor tissue was preserved with small necrosis foci. Conclusions: The study showed that recombinant hybrid protein of tumor necrosis factor-alphathymosin-alpha1 in addition to the conventional chemotherapy had a significant damaging effect on the tumor due to its cytotoxic and anti-angiogenic action.
