N2  - Purpose/Objective(s): MoststudiesexaminingtreatingpatientswithStageIII nonesmall cell lung cancer (NSCLC) include 3 histologic types: adenocarcinoma, and squamous cell and large cell carcinoma. Currently, the 2 most often used chemotherapy regimens concurrent with radiation in these patients is Carboplatin/Paclitaxel and Cisplatin/Etoposide (1,2). To date, studies incorporating patients with Stage III NSCLC are enrolled regardless of histology type. Nonetheless, advances in genomic data show that patients with NSCLC with different histology have distinct gene expression profiles and driver mutations that further distinguish adenocarcinoma from squamous cell carcinoma (3). Moreover, data from a landmark study showed an improvement in both PFS &OS by treating patients with Stage IV NSCLC based on tumor histology (4). For these reasons, our team asked the question if treating patients with Stage III NSCLC according to histologic type had any difference in terms of response rate, PFS, or OS. Materials/Methods: We performed a retrospective review of records at our institution to identify patients with NSCLC who received chemoradiation therapy from 2009-2015. A total of 126 patients were identified and 37 out of these met our inclusion criteria. Most of these patients received either Carboplatin/Paclitaxel or Cisplatin/Etoposide concurrent with radiation, either as induction followed by surgery or as definitive chemoradiation therapy. We then examined the outcomes of this patient cohort in response rate, PFS, and OS around stratification of tumor histology, squamous versus non-squamous. Results: Our team did not identify a difference in response rate in patients with Stage III NSCLC treated with chemoradiation therapy based on tumor histology (squamous vs non-squamous), 57.14% vs 42.86% (P=.9181). We also did not identify a difference in either PFS (HR: 0.524; 95% CI: 0.200-1.370) or OS (HR: 0.693; 95% CI: 0.184-2.599) based on tumor histology (squamous vs non-squamous). Conclusion: Our review did not identify a statistical difference in terms of response rate, PFS, or OS in patients with Stage III NSCLC treated with neoadjuvant or definitive chemoradiation therapy amongst tumor histology (squamous vs non-squamous). Furthermore, data from a phase 3 trial comparing the 2 chemotherapy regimens (Pemetrexed/Cisplatin vs Etoposide/Cisplatin) in patients with locally advanced non-squamous NSCLC did not identify a difference in outcomes (5). Consequently, given the overall poor prognosis of this patient population treated with chemoradiation therapy regardless of histology or chemotherapy regimen used, new innovative approaches to treatment are essential in improving future outcomes. Potential approaches might include interval imaging to assess disease response to guide whether to change chemotherapy regimens and effectively integrating the use of newer targeted agents and immunotherapy in treatment of this patient population.
